{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '100,310p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '100,310p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"```\n\n`:completion` is a new discriminated structural row. Its descriptor table is:\n\n| Completion tag | Slots after the tag | Restored engine meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:next` | `C` | Ordinary completed reader; reconstruct cursor-ref and stream-id through the census. |\n| `:put` | `stream-cref, datom-V, optional-op-id-D` | Ordinary completed append outcome; never append again. |\n| `:ffi` | `C, call-id-D, op-D` | Completed FFI response reader; restore engine reason `:next` and correlation fields. |\n| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D, envelope-V, request-stream-cref, response-stream-cref, C, optional-op-id-D` | Completed request append; restore `:request-sent true`, so successful resume creates the response wait rather than delivering the append result to the guest. |\n| `:link-request` | `link-id-D, module-S, try?-D, optional-op-id-D` | Completed link-request outcome; no outstanding request or response transport is required. |\n| `:link-response` | `link-id-D, module-S, try?-D` | Completed link-response outcome. |\n| `:install` | `module-S, try?-D` | Completed initializer outcome; no remaining install entry is required. |\n\nThe existing `pending-table` remains the grammar of **outstanding** operations. `completion-table` is distinct. The `:ffi` and `:ffi-request` wire tags preserve the existing distinction between engine `:next` and `:put` entries carrying FFI correlation.\n\nAll existing queue producers are covered. `wake-installed` queues install completions, and `poll-links` queues link outcomes. A completion must not reconstruct a retired request envelope, response cursor or install solely to satisfy pending validation.\n\nFor link/install success, status may be absent: it is encoded canonically as nil. A try failure travels in `:V` as ordinary returned outcome data. An ordinary require failure carries status `:link-refused` and its refusal value in the last slot; restore reinstates `:refusal` so the existing terminal-resume path raises the same error. The refusal slot is absent for other outcomes. Status is preserved, not inferred from the delivered value.\n\nThe redundant queue-entry `:cursor` is not carried. Its operative resource updates are carried explicitly. Empty or nil update maps have one spelling: an empty update row.\n\n**Frame and completion validation.**\n\n| Rule | Requirement | Diagnostic |\n|---|---|---|\n| S1 | States are `:act`, `:tail`, `:run`; a run has exactly state, segment, pc, env, window and k. | `:frame-shape` |\n| C2 | Exact integer PC; act and return-frame PCs are positive and in bounds; run PC may be zero. | `:pc-kind`, `:pc-bounds` |\n| C3 | Act and return frames retain body continuity; the run itself skips that predecessor check. | `:pc-body` |\n| R1 | Run window keys are in the body’s register scope. | `:window-scope` |\n| R2 | Run window keys equal `L(pc)`; act/return windows retain `saved(pc, rd)`. | `:window-extra`, `:window-missing` |\n| A1–A6, T1 | Existing delivery-state rules remain for act/tail; skipped for run. | Existing diagnostics |\n| P1 | `:wait` requires pending; `:captured`, `:parked`, `:running`, `:ready` require pending absent. Run is admitted only in `:running`; `:ready` admits only act/tail. | `:pending-presence` |\n| E1, E2, K1–K3, X1 | Existing environment, continuation and task-context checks remain. | Existing diagnostics |\n| Q1 | A ready completion’s tag must be compatible with the delivery site, using the same site/reason relation as waits. | `:reason-mismatch` |\n| Q2 | Link/install completion `try?` is boolean; completed install does not require membership in the task’s installs. | `:pending-try` |\n| Q3 | Ready status is nil or a keyword; `:link-refused` requires link/install completion, `try? false`, and refusal data; other outcomes have no refusal slot. | `:ready-outcome` |\n| Q4 | Every update key names a cell in this task’s slot-9 census; update rows have unique keys and valid bodies. | Existing missing-cell/duplicate-key diagnostics |\n| Q5 | Every present completed operation ID passes shape-only validation. | `:malformed-op-id`, `:inexact` |\n\nQ1 and Q2 receive the completion descriptor through the validation context; they do not fabricate a pending field on the delivery frame. Ready FFI descriptors contribute their call IDs to task context alongside outstanding FFI waits, so parked FFI records remain valid while their response or sent-request outcome is queued.\n\n**Custody by kind.**\n\n| Site | Required behavior |\n|---|---|\n| `task-ops`, `custody-baseline`, `check-root-custody!` | Admit `:running` beside the existing kinds. |\n| `header-custody` | Running exclusive roots take the existing four-key arm, plus origin when present. |\n| `check-pendings!` | Validate task cardinalities, outstanding waits, installs, and completed descriptors through separate paths. |\n| `check-pendings!`, slot 18 | Invoke a factored `check-op-id-shape!` for every present completed operation ID. |\n| Existing `check-op-id!` | Call that shape helper, then retain origin, occurrence and counter checks for retained writes. |\n| `task-ops`, `protect!`, `with-custody` numbering | Continue to operate on outstanding writes; completed outcomes are excluded. |\n| `check-custody!` | Preserve the fenced/halted grant exemption. |\n| `check-install!` | Admit blocked child/parked phase and running child/running phase. |\n| `lower-task` | Preserve exclusive grant/protection requirements for non-fenced running roots. |\n\n`check-op-id-shape!` checks the existing two-field operation-ID shape, valid occurrence and exact sequence representation. It does not require root origin, compare the historical occurrence to the current root, or impose the current root’s sequence frontier. Nil remains the canonical absence of an ID for ungated operations.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P1-3; P2-5; glm N4/N5; terminal-status and FFI-context consequences of completed-state validation.\n\n### 3.2 Lift, lower, restore\n\n**Replaces v2 §3.2 completely.**\n\n**Lift, `lift-task*`:**\n\n1. Refuse the existing holds through `unliftable-hold`. Remove the ready-queue refusal.\n2. Classify kind by §3.1, preserving running → parked → blocked → halted precedence.\n3. Build the run from control, environment, K and `select-keys` of the current window over `L(pc)`; register its code/environment context for discharge.\n4. Build outstanding waits through the existing pending path.\n5. Build each ready entry through a separate completion path: delivery-state node, completion descriptor, lifted outcome value, optional status, explicit resource-update row and optional lifted refusal.\n6. Validate run frames in `:running`, ready delivery frames in `:ready`, and their completion descriptors through Q1–Q5. Outstanding waits remain in `:wait`.\n7. Preserve the existing store, parked-table, module-store, dependency discharge, module-pin and child-admission behavior.\n\n**Cursor identity and update-body encoding.**\n\nSlot 9 is the task’s sole cursor-cell census. `cell-for!` registers each referenced resource identity with its **current machine resource body**. On the shared-cursor sweep path, that body is already the final post-sweep body. Do not describe it as a pre-sweep position.\n\nFor each ready resource update:\n\n- Register or resolve the same resource identity through the task census.\n- Emit the update’s own `:cursor-cell-body` node directly from its stream and position.\n- Pair that node with the same canonical C in the ready update row.\n- Do not register the update body through a second `cell!` call.\n\nThus one C may be paired with different bodies across slot 9 and ready rows, or with equal bodies that Merkle sharing coalesces. Neither case creates a second cell identity.\n\nThe codec must distinguish **the census at task slot 9** from **update rows referenced by ready entries**. Existing blanket skips of every `:cursor-cells` row are not sufficient. Audit cell discovery, canonical numbering, emission and decoded validation: skip the census as a source of ordinary identity occurrences, but traverse ready update keys and bodies as structural references. Numbering uses the task census’s identities; update rows never establish a separate census. Missing census membership and duplicate keys refuse before attachment.\n\n**Lower/restore.**\n\nA running task restores:\n\n```clojure\n{:control {:segment local :pc pc}\n :window W\n :env E\n :k (when (seq K) K)\n :halted? false\n :blocked? false\n :value nil\n :wait-set entries}\n```\n\nEvery kind restores slot 18 in order. Resolve the ready state and completion descriptor, reconstruct the engine entry described in §3.1, then restore its value, status, refusal and resource updates. Update keys map through the slot-9 C→fresh-resource-key map; update bodies come from the ready row, not from slot 9.\n\nNo completed operation is polled or reissued by lower. A completed FFI request append resumes through the existing response-wait transition. Link/install completion restores without resurrecting retired pending resources.\n\nA fenced restore retains ready entries in `:yin.k/recovery-ready`, alongside recovery waits and parked records, and exposes an empty scheduler queue. Running control remains available for byte-identical relift, protected by the 6d-a closed-gate guard.\n\n**Backend refusal traversal.**\n\n`lower-defect` inspects the slot-17 run through `state-prim`. For each slot-18 row it explicitly resolves the first cref to its delivery-state row, then calls `state-prim` on that row. It also calls `value-prim` on the outcome `:V` and optional refusal value. Any value-bearing completion fields that will be restored as backend payload receive the same traversal.\n\nThis replaces v2’s double-dereference expression. With the corrected grammar there is no intermediate wait row to dereference.\n\nThe independent test oracle traverses the same semantic roots independently of implementation helpers. Existing image-collision, free-environment, install and retained-FFI backend limits remain. Unsupported ready values must refuse before attachment.\n\n**Export classification.**\n\n`export/kind-of` follows the complete §3.1 precedence, including ready-only blocked tasks. Recovery metadata and validity checks include the ordered ready list. Export/abort/reopen preserve the list rather than executing it.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-4 adopts glm N1’s independent update-body encoding and muse N2’s shared-cursor interpretation; P2-7 and glm N2 fix ready-value traversal and cref resolution.\n\n### 3.3 Custody and lifecycle of a running root\n\n**Replace outcome 2, outcome 4 and the tests paragraph; retain outcomes 1 and 3 and explicit hand-off policy.**\n\n**2. Tenure dies after a completed slice.**\n\n`run-cycle` becomes a sequence of stage-local transitions. A stage receives the latest holder state and returns either a completed successor state or an explicit failure outcome containing the latest completed state.\n\n```text\nstage-result =\n  {:status :ok,     :state completed-state}\n  or\n  {:status :failed, :state latest-completed-state, :failure failure}\n```\n\nEach stage:\n\n1. Checks tenure against its input state.\n2. Executes its operation, retaining the existing tenure checks at external seams.\n3. Associates a successfully returned machine and stage metadata into a successor state.\n4. Passes that successor to the next stage.\n\nThe stage-local catch closes over that stage’s input state. A later tenure failure therefore returns the state containing the previous stage’s completed machine. It does not fall back to `step-active`’s original argument.\n\nThe failure consumer calls `end-run` with the state carried in the failure outcome. `end-run` retains its machine, gates it `:ended`, and records `:yin.k/retained-boundary` diagnostics. Journal-uncertain and inbox-refusal exceptions retain their existing propagation rules; they are not converted into ordinary program failures.\n\nIf an operation throws before returning a machine, retain the last completed stage, not an invented partial result. External effects already performed are not rolled back. No retained boundary receives new execution or publication authority.\n\n**4. Reopen and fencing.**\n\nA fenced running restore keeps control, window, environment and K, with gate `:exporting`. The kernel entry guard is already present from **6d-a**. It refuses `:exporting` and `:ended` before executing an instruction or entering a scheduler/bridge path.\n\nDirect `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel` are covered. The guard remains outside the instruction loop. `rehydrate-fenced` must reproduce the published pack byte for byte, including ordered completed outcomes and cursor updates.\n\n**Tests, 6d-b.**\n\nRetain the five clock-controlled cuts from v2: before the slice, immediately after it, during replay/emit/read, between slices, and during running-root export. At each cut assert the **exact retained machine**, not merely its PC or the presence of diagnostics. A failure inside a stage retains its input machine; a failure after a completed stage retains its output machine.\n\nKeep the source fenced or ended, forbid effects after tenure loss, and verify either an authorized successor or recovery from the last durable checkpoint. Retain competing-candidate, uncertain-abort, expired-grant and recovery-byte-equality cases. Repeat the 6d-a execution-entry fencing checks through the holder’s reopened object.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-8 supplies an explicit exception-state channel; P1-2 moves fencing into 6d-a while retaining lifecycle tests in 6d-b.\n\n### 3.4 Fuel and budget propagation\n\n**Replaces v2 §3.4 completely.**\n\n**`yin.vm.semantic/run-fuel [machine n]`**, public, with positive exact integer `n`.\n\nOne invocation performs:\n\n1. Closed-gate check.\n2. Exactly one raw budgeted action:\n   - If control is present, `vm-hot` with fuel `n`.\n   - Otherwise, one budgeted scheduler round.\n3. If the result is blocked and an explicit bridge is installed, at most one `ffi/bridge-step`.\n4. Return that resulting machine immediately.\n\nThe implementation does **not** wrap the raw action in unchanged `ffi/maybe-run`. A handled response or malformed-request diagnostic does not restart the runner or bridge inside the same invocation. The next caller invocation resumes progress.\n\nThe bounded bridge helper may be private to the semantic runner or a bounded variant beside `ffi/maybe-run`; its contract is the four steps above. Existing unrestricted `vm/run` bridge behavior remains unchanged for live machines.\n\n**Budgeted scheduler round.**\n\nPropagate the budget through `check-wait-set`, `poll-links`, `advance-installs` and `advance-install`. Each visited child is invoked once through `run-fuel child n`; nested children use the same rule. Preserve existing gate stamping and the prohibition on running children under closed gates.\n\nAfter the sweep, resume at most one queued entry. Do not immediately run the resumed activation in that invocation. A running child retains phase `:running`; blocked children retain `:parked`; halted children complete installation as today.\n\nThe dispatch tests **control presence**, not merely `engine/active-continuation?`: the latter only tests blocked/halted flags and can classify a control-less ingress-ready machine as active. Therefore no `run-fuel` path falls through `vm-hot` into its unbudgeted scheduler fallback. A quiescent control-less machine takes the budgeted no-work round and returns.\n\n**Per-tree bound.**\n\nEach task visited during the invocation receives at most one budgeted action and at most one bridge step. Each task executes at most `n` guest instructions. For `t` distinct tasks visited during the invocation, including children created during the round, the instruction bound is `n × t`. No task is revisited with a fresh budget inside that invocation.\n\n**The honest guarantee**, copied verbatim into the documents:\n\n> Fuel counts completed guest instructions. One `run-fuel` invocation executes at most `n` instructions per visited task, at most one scheduler round per visited inactive task, and at most one bridge step per visited task. A bridge result never restarts execution inside that invocation. No wall-clock bound is claimed for an instruction, host function, stream constructor, variable resolution, bridge step or scheduler sweep. The VM has no clock and converts no time to instructions; `dao.lease` owns liveness and the composition chooses `n`.\n\n`vm/step` keeps its telemetry behavior. `run-fuel` adds no per-fuel snapshot; existing park, resume and bridge telemetry remains.\n\nRunning install children use the same carrier and receiver-admission checks as other tasks. The backends continue to refuse install children.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P1-1 bounds bridge execution; glm’s inactive-predicate omission is closed explicitly.\n\n### 3.5 Stack layouts at control-flow PCs; the register side\n\n**Replace v2’s displayed ordered equation and its explanatory paragraph. Retain jump/terminator derivation, stutter normalization, dead-prefix treatment and register allocation requirements.**\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
