t fixture fails the equality branch loudly. Jump/terminator layouts (`layout.cljc` `stack-layouts`: `result-rd` through the consequent, `layout(j) = pending ++ [rd(if)]`, terminator `[]`) are in; no dead-prefix lift, no `slice-window` widening, no running-backend lift anywhere in the diff. §3.5's deferral sentence and §8.6 item 9's text are in the continuation doc word-for-word.\n- **Ruling 2 (leading-refusal exclusion)** — the general rule is implemented exactly, and `discharge!` is untouched (no hunk in its region). `check-returned-boundaries` throws on any refusal after the first successful lift, asserts contiguity (every prefix of each excluded point is excluded), non-empty obligations, zero serves at every excluded boundary, an extra nil-serve discriminator (`(= refusal d)`, proving the refusal is not serve-dependent), obligations unanswered at each excluded boundary and answered at the first measured one, and per-case `[measured excluded park halt]` counts pinned (quick 23-case table + the 89-case slow table). Row 20 is pinned to the measured set; row 17's obligations pinned to exactly `#{host.mod/f}`.\n- **Ruling 3 (ledger golden)** — all four conditions met: regenerated through `ledger-fixtures/render` only; the field-classified inventory (`target/6da/r2-ledger-diff-inventory.edn`) classifies `:address-only`; the six frame-byte equalities are accounted (journal frames 2, 9, 11, 13–15, every change at a `:segment/blake3-…` path; my own inspection of the diff's ±pairs confirms the lines differ only in blake3 segments; the 14 other frames byte-identical; the standalone worked-frame pin unmoved); digests recorded. The 19 inline pins all match `root-remints.txt` pair-for-pair (I checked all 19 across `rows_test.cljc`).\n- **v2.1 change list** — all present and faithful: `completion-table` exactly as specified; `:ready`/`:run` row grammar and task slots 17/18 with the 19-slot decode gate; the census role-aware traversal (the three former blanket `:cursor-cells` skips at rows.cljc discovery/numbering/spelling/decode now skip only the task's slot 9, so update rows are numbered, traversed and membership-checked — the P2-4 mechanism, with `ready-node` emitting the update's own `:cursor-cell-body` under the census's `cell-for!` identity, restore reading bodies from the ready row through `cell-keys`); `check-op-id-shape!` factored and called from slot 18 with correct per-tag op-id indices; `check-install!`'s phase↔kind pairing; `with-custody`'s `:yin.k/recovery-ready`; the running restore arm; `check-gate!` at `vm-hot`/`vm/step`/`vm/run`/`run-fuel` with a private `vm-hot*` raw runner (one check, outside the loop); `run-fuel`'s control-presence dispatch, single budgeted action, at most one `bridge-step`, no `maybe-run` wrap; budgeted `run-child` threading through `check-wait-set`→`poll-links`→`advance-installs`→`advance-install` preserving the closed-gate skip; Q1–Q5 via the completion-in-context mechanism with FFI call-ids gathered from completions too.\n- **Zero-serve/zero-attach discipline (c)** — held everywhere I checked: every new lower-refusal probe asserts `(is (zero? @n))`; `check-window-tampers` asserts the diagnostic **and** a refusal with zero attaches at every measured boundary; lift refusals count serves and assert zero; the lift's new **preflight pass** (temporary plain-data identities before any real serve) makes \"a discharge/portability refusal serves nothing\" structural, and the docs' §7.1 clause added for it is accurate. No assertion was removed to make stopped cases green; the two replaced refusal tests (`a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-carried`, the dropped \"not at a safepoint\" case) are the plan's own §3.8-authorized positive replacements.\n- **Per-point law 1 (item 7/§3.6 step 5)** — implemented, via a mechanism worth naming for Fable: at each execution target the observer *returns the receiver*, so `fuel-drive` continues driving the lowered machine on the replay world's shared streams to the reference outcome (`\"isolated law 1\"` — sampled structural points for 22 rows, every boundary for rows 1/20 and in the slow `:all` run, every measured boundary in the products). Not vacuous.\n- **Docs (d)** — the owned §3.7 clauses match v2.1's text; the §3.8 sign-off checklist holds: no normative clause assigns the guard to 6d-b (the §7.3 sentence correctly assigns stage-local holder transitions to 6d-b), none forbids completed link/install wakes, none validates completed installs against live installs, none restarts fuel through `maybe-run`, none promises one-cycle hold resolution; opus's three extra doc fixes are the §7.6.3/dependency-completion/tuples stale-clause markers, correctly scoped; the deliberately-left items (UCF Quiesce §7.7.4/§7.8 → 6d-b, historical wire maps, linker-DHT) are the right calls.\n\n## Findings (all P3 — nothing P0–P2 found)\n\n1. **P3 — vacuous repeated-register pin.** `layout_test.cljc` `executed-ordered-regions` closes with `(is (<= 0 @repeated))` — true even if no repeated-register group ever occurs, so the duplicate-operand agreement check is exercised (the corpus's `(+ x x)` guarantees a group) but not *pinned*. Fix: `(is (pos? @repeated))`.\n2. **P3 — likely-dead store-keys clause.** `safepoint_harness_test.cljc` `boundary-description`'s registry clause looks up `(get (:module-stores m) (:address entry))`, but machine `:module-stores` is keyed by module *name* (cf. the existing `:module-stores {'scoped {}}` fixtures), so the get probably misses and the clause contributes nothing (the install-manifest clause carries row 17 today). Verify and either key it correctly or drop it, so the \"answered later\" oracle doesn't silently depend on the install clause alone.\n3. **P3 — singular/plural and one re-wording in the acceptance text** (the doc consequences of the two flagged items below): item 7's \"the leading boundary is excluded\" should read \"the leading **boundaries** are excluded\", and the obligations clause should read \"…names the case's own execution later answers — store or environment names its own later definitions execute, **or module exports its own `require` installs**\". One-line doc edits in `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md` §8.6.\n4. **P3 — scope observations for Fable's diff-confirm** (not defects; all tested green): (i) the lift now runs `lift-task*` twice on success (preflight with fake identities + real pass) — a ~2× lift cost purchased for structural zero-serves, documented in §7.1; (ii) `restore-task` now re-receives the receiver's registry modules through `engine/receive-module` after restore so lowered bindings name the restored coordinates — a restore-semantics addition beyond v2.1's letter; (iii) the harness composition widens `:primitives` with the stream module's functions (`portable-stream-primitives`) so mid-application operator values lift as `:prim` rows — legitimate for any-pc windows, but it changes every harness machine; (iv) `run-beside` now resets `:env` to `:free-env` on the semantic lane.\n\n## The two flagged items\n\n**(i) Row 20's excluded set — the implementation is right; my ruling's example was wrong about the count. CONFIRMED as implemented; example corrected.** The setup batch's first `s/def!` stores `s` only after pcs 0–2, so the leading refusal run is `[pc 0, pc 1, pc 2] × #{s}` — three contiguous boundaries, each refusing `[:yin.k/unsatisfied :incomplete]` with obligations exactly `#{s}` and zero serves, independently pinned by `conservative-prefixes-refuse-before-serving` (n = 0, 1, 2, each at pc = n). Ruling 2's *general rule* — contiguous leading run, obligations recorded and non-empty, zero serves, hard failure after the first success, counts reported separately — is what the code implements, exactly; only my illustrative `[S₀, obligations #{s}]` under-counted. Apply the plural re-wording in finding 3.\n\n**(ii) Row 17 as a \"like case\" — CONFIRMED, with the re-wording.** The obligations are `#{host.mod/f}` on every excluded boundary (pinned exactly), answered once the case's own `require` of `host.mod` installs — the module's manifest exports are part of what the case's own execution later provides, no external dependency is missing, and the same conservative-discharge family (Q6, derive-don't-persist) applies. My ruling's phrase \"names the case's own program defines later\" does not literally cover require-answered exports; read it as \"names the case's own execution later answers\", and land the item-7 re-wording in finding 3 so the doc says what is pinned. Note row 17 `:captured` excludes 43 vs 27 measured — legitimate (the wrapper's own leading boundaries all precede the require completing), and the contiguity/answered-at-first-measured assertions are what make it safe, not the ratio.\n\n## What the landing gates must watch\n\n1. **`with-redefs` on the Dart lane — the top risk.** The new `.cljc` tests introduce ~10 `with-redefs` sites (including redefining `vm/run`, private `sr/vm-hot*` via `@#'`, `engine/scheduler-round-fuel`, `ffi/bridge-step`, `stream/append!`, `stream/next`). The repo has exact precedent for this breaking the cljd lane — commit `51117e93` (\"injectable publish seam … drop with-redefs\") exists for that reason. If the Dart lane fails to compile or silently misbehaves on these, the fix is reader-condition gating or seam injection, not lane suppression. The Node lane's `with-redefs` over vars is expected to work but must be confirmed in the log.\n2. **Cross-host boundary-count equality.** `quick-boundary-pins` and the 89-case slow table are JVM-measured. If Node or Dart measure different `[measured excluded park halt]` vectors for any case, that is a cross-host determinism **finding** to report — not a fixture to remint per host.\n3. **Slow-tier duration on Dart**: 129,274 assertions at the JVM's pace suggests a long Dart run — budget it against the 2-hour background limit used for this subsystem's slow lanes, and run it once, not per iteration.\n4. **Namespace coverage in the Node/Dart logs**: confirm `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test`, `layout-test`, `vm-test`, `frame-test`, `engine-test`, `rows-test`, `rows-custody-test`, `ledger-test` all appear and run.\n5. The orchestrator's JVM quick-tier re-run should match the report's 4,079/269,020/0 baseline; any delta localizes to unreviewed drift since `r3-quick.log`.\n\n**Bottom line:** the diff is a faithful, frequently stronger-than-required implementation of v2.1 and all three rulings, with honest evidence artifacts; I found no correctness defect and no weakened assertion — four P3 nits, two of which are the flagged items' one-line doc consequences. Approve with nits; Fable's Tuesday diff-confirm remains the merge gate, with the `with-redefs`/Dart question the one item that could still force a rework before landing.\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-16 14:07:54 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-16 21:07:54 +07\nCoding-Agent: cmd\nSession-ID: 828a1b0d-c2f7-4204-8920-6ca11607a772\n# Task: independent diff review of slice 6d-a — cmd moonshotai/kimi-k3 verdict\nRole: Reviewer\nImplementers:\n- Model: moonshotai/kimi-k3 | Assigned: 2026-10-16 19:46 +07 | Status: completed | Rationale: fresh third-family reviewer; turn 1 read the full diff (1.22M input tokens) but was cut off by sandbox denials on worktree reads; resumed once in its home project scope (stdout3.log) and delivered; final message promoted verbatim below; stdout at collab/1792160000000-reviewer-6da-diff-cmd.kimi-k3.stdout{,2,3}.log\n\nAll verifications are complete — `header-custody`'s body already routes non-halted kinds (including `:running`) to the four-key arm, `protect!` scans only slot 13 (completed outcomes excluded by construction), and every helper the new code calls (`ffi/installed?`, `code/reg?`, `cell-for!`, `stream-row!`, the `restore-task` letfn locals, `resume-from-run-queue`, `halted-with-empty-queue?`, `module-entries`) pre-exists in the base tree I could read. Delivering the review.\n\n6DA-DIFF-REVIEW: APPROVE-WITH-NITS\n\nScope read completely: the full 363KB diff (docs, engine/semantic/frame/layout/rows src, both fixtures, all seven test files), plan v2.1, the three glm rulings, and the opus completion report. I independently re-derived the load-bearing base facts from the readable base tree (no ns-require hunks exist in the diff, so base aliases govern): `yin.vm.semantic` already aliases `code` and `ffi` (semantic.cljc:7,11), so `code/reg?` and `ffi/installed?` resolve; rows.cljc pre-defines `stream-row!` (:1357), `cell!` (:1373), `cell-for!` (:1381) and requires `analysis` (:28); machine code images carry `:analysis` (semantic.cljc:300), so the lift's `(get-in m [:code segment :analysis])` is sound; `restore-task`'s letfn locals `value`/`payload`/`entry`/`stream-key` exist, and `entry`'s pending-slot layouts for `:next`/`:put`/`:ffi`/`:ffi-request` are identical to the new `completion-table`'s — which is exactly why wrapping a completion as a fake `:wait` row for restore is sound, and why `:link-*`/`:install` (whose wire shapes differ from pendings) get their own restore arm; base `vm-hot` falls to unbudgeted `scheduler-round` when control and k are both nil (semantic.cljc:137-138), and `run-fuel`'s control-presence dispatch never reaches `vm-hot*` without control, so the spec's \"no fall-through into the unbudgeted scheduler fallback\" holds by construction; `resume-from-run-queue` (engine.cljc:2280) pops exactly one entry, merges updates, runs the terminal check, and restores without executing; `header-custody`'s unchanged body routes every non-`:halted` kind (so `:running`) to the four-key arm (rows.cljc:1713-1716); `protect!` scans slot 13 only, so completed outcomes are excluded from protection/enrollment by construction.\n\n(a) Fidelity. The v2.1 change list is implemented point-for-point: completion-table (diff 1390-1397) matches §3.1's descriptor table tag-for-tag; task slots 17/18 and the run/ready rows (1407-1418); role-aware census traversal that skips only task slot 9 instead of every