ut (spot-checked: task-products 3948-3951, completion-try 4318-4321/4332-4335, put-history 4267-4271, slot-matrix 4540-4544, install-child 4660-4663, fenced counters 4419-4428, row-20 exclusions 4080); no pass-by-construction or weakened assertion found; `executed-ordered-regions`' `(is (<= 0 @repeated))` (diff 3703) is vacuous but harmless, sitting beside real ordered/repeated-register equality checks.\n- P3-1: continuation doc §5.7 ends with the plan-amendment line \"**Change:** Consensus P2-7; muse N4/glm O3; glm's terminal-status acceptance omission.\" (diff 356) — spec-edition vocabulary inside normative design text, inherited from copying v2.1 §3.6 verbatim. Minimal fix: drop the line.\n- P3-2: every any-pc amendment marker cites \"owner invariant 2026-10-17\" while being dated 2026-10-16 (diff 34, 72, 612, etc.). Confirm the citation date at landing; likely a timestamp artifact, but the design docs are the historical record.\n- P3-3: the gate guard's refusal shape `{:rule :gate-closed :yin.k/gate mode}` (diff 1102-1106) is engine-consistent but new and unnamed in the docs; tests pin `:gate-closed`. Name it in continuation §7.3/7.4 so 6d-b doesn't re-invent it.\n- P3-4: lift-side `(boolean (:try? e))` coercion in `completion-node` (diff 1667-1669) means Q2's boolean teeth exist only at lower; acceptable since the engine is the trusted producer, but a one-line comment would prevent a future \"fix\" from weakening it.\n- P3-5: `lift-task` now runs `lift-task*` twice per call (preflight, diff 1843-1852). Deliberate and documented (zero external serves on refusal); flag as a cost note for the 6d-b holder lanes, which lift far more often.\n- P3-6 (flagged item 1, see below): doc keeps the ruling-verbatim singular \"the leading boundary\" (diff 559) while the pin is three boundaries.\n- P3-7: the receiver rebinding via `engine/receive-module` in `restore-task` (diff 1980-1987, doc-synced at 449-453) is outside v2.1's letter. It is tested (byte-equal relift on a two-module tree in `long-and-nested-initializer-transfers`; module resolution asserted on the receiver), but Fable's diff-confirm should acknowledge it as intentional.\n\n(c) The two flagged items.\n- Row 20, three boundaries vs the ruling's `[S₀]`: the implementation is correct and the ruling's example was a prediction. The ruling's operative rule — excludable only while no boundary of the same case has lifted, contiguous leading run, obligations answered later, zero serves — and acceptance item 7's own criterion (\"lifts only from the first boundary at which its definitions are answered in the store\") both define the correct behavior, and the measurement satisfies them: pcs 0, 1 and 2 each refuse `[:yin.k/unsatisfied :incomplete]` with obligations exactly `#{s}` and zero serves; pc 3 onward lifts; `discharge!` is untouched; `conservative-prefixes-refuse-before-serving` pins the same three prefixes independently; the new assertions prove cause (`s` absent at each excluded boundary, present at the first measured one). Pinning the measured triple instead of the predicted singleton is the right call — a one-boundary pin would have forced a fake. The only residue is the doc's singular wording; re-word \"the leading boundary\" to \"the leading run\" in a docs pass, or have the acting architect confirm the measured pin supersedes the example (the ruling's general rule already covers it, so I consider this confirmed-in-substance).\n- Row 17 as a like case: I confirm it. The ruling's substantive criterion is that the obligation is established later by the case's own program and answered at the first non-excluded boundary, and the test verifies that mechanically: `boundary-description`'s `:store-keys` now counts installing modules' manifest exports (matching `discharge!`'s answer order), the general assertion requires every excluded boundary's obligations to be a subset of the union of later boundaries' keys, and row 17 gets its own assertion that every exclusion carries exactly `#{'host.mod/f}`. A `require`'d module's exports are as much \"the case's own program defines later\" as a store `def!` — both are conservative-discharge obligations the program itself establishes (default 5's family, whose existing precedent is the pre-branch `ghost` pin). One caveat worth recording for 6d-b: the `:store-keys` accounting re-implements discharge's answer set; if `discharge!` ever gains another obligation source, this check could drift silently — the durable fix is to derive the answer set from `discharge!` itself rather than re-listing it in the harness.\n\n(d) What the landing gates should watch.\n1. The unrun lanes are the critical path: Node and Dart quick tiers plus the three-host slow tier (acceptance 14). Every pin table here is labeled \"measured on JVM 2026-10-16\"; counts are deterministic per the corpus, so a cross-host count divergence is a host-nondeterminism signal to investigate, not to waive.\n2. Fable's Tuesday diff-confirm carries four specific artifacts: the codex-authored hunks (unreviewed by a non-author family), the ledger inventory `target/6da/r2-ledger-diff-inventory.edn` (verify the `:address-only` classification and that the six frame-byte equalities each embed a moved address), the retained-FFI fixture correction (`:call-id` now `:parked-0`, an assertion-target fix, kept flagged), and the two items in (c).\n3. The `receive-module` rebinding (P3-7) needs an explicit intentionality acknowledgment; it is the one behavioral change in restore with no v2.1 line item.\n4. 6d-b must still replace UCF §7.7.4's Quiesce step 1 (deliberately deferred) — v2.1 §3.8's pre-sign-off hygiene check depends on it, and the guard must not be re-assigned there (v2.1 6d-b item 5).\n5. Keep the zero-serve/zero-attach assertion pattern mandatory in 6d-b's stage-cut tests — acceptance there (\"assert the exact retained machine, not merely its PC\") is the same failure mode this slice guarded against.\n6. The two doc nits (P3-1, P3-2) and the guard-diagnostic naming (P3-3) fit in one small docs-follow-up commit; none blocks landing.\n7. Re-verify kondo on all 13 changed files at landing (engineer reports 0/0; independent confirmation is one command).\n8. The orchestrator's re-run of the JVM quick tier should be diffed against `r3-quick.log` (4,079 tests / 269,020 assertions / 0 failures) — identical counts expected, since src has been frozen since 19:17 per the mutation-campaign timestamp.\n6DA-FABLE-CONFIRM: SIGNED-OFF (head d5987015)\n\n**What I verified myself, not taken from the reviewers or engineers.** The staged diff file is byte-identical to `git diff b9103797..d5987015`. I read every source hunk in semantic, frame, layout, engine and rows, the full harness and test diffs, and the full docs diff. clj-kondo on all 13 changed Clojure files reports 0 errors, 0 warnings. I ran the JVM quick tier for the seven touched test namespaces in the worktree:\n\n| Run | Tests | Assertions | Failures |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| frame, layout, vm, rows, rows-custody, engine, safepoint-harness | 204 | 24,244 | 0 |\n\nThe printed measurements match the pinned tables. I did not rerun Node, Dart or the slow tier; for those I rely on the orchestrator's logged runs.\n\n**Per-point rulings**\n\n1. **Fidelity to plan v2.1: CONFIRMED.** The completion table, slots 17 and 18, the run row, the 19-slot task gate, ready-context frame judging with the descriptor passed through the task context, the factored `check-op-id-shape!` called from slot 18 with per-tag op-id indices, the census traversal that now skips only task slot 9 at all four sites, ready-row traversal in `lower-defect` including value-bearing completion fields, jump and terminator layouts, budget propagation through check-wait-set, poll-links, advance-install(s) via a run-child parameter, `scheduler-round-fuel` with at most one resume, `run-fuel` dispatching on control presence with one raw action and at most one bridge step and no `maybe-run` wrap, and `check-gate!` on vm-hot, step, run and run-fuel outside the loop via a private raw runner. All match §3.1, §3.2, §3.4 and the 6d-a change list. The §3.8 hygiene check passes: no normative clause assigns the guard to 6d-b, forbids completed link/install wakes, validates completed installs against live installs, restarts fuel through `maybe-run`, or promises one-cycle hold resolution.\n2. **Ledger golden: CONFIRMED by my own byte check.** Decoding each changed hex line, every differing byte lies inside a 64-character hex run immediately preceded by the CBOR text header and `segment` and `blake3-`. Nine digests change across journal frames 2, 9, 11, 13, 14 and 15, matching the inventory's entries. The projection line changes as a consequence. Classification `:address-only` stands.\n3. **Retained-FFI `:call-id` correction: CONFIRMED.** The completed-ffi-append test asserts the restored response wait carries the source wait's actual call id and that the request stream was not appended a second time. That is the right assertion target.\n4. **Flagged items: both CONFIRMED.** Row 20 measures exactly pcs 0, 1, 2 with obligations `#{s}`, independently pinned by the conservative-prefixes test, and the general rule in `check-returned-boundaries` is what governs. Row 17 is a like case: every excluded boundary carries exactly `#{host.mod/f}`, answered at the first measured boundary. The doc wording already uses the plural and the require-answered clause. cmd's caveat about the harness re-listing discharge's answer set is a real drift risk; carry it as the 6d-b note already drafted.\n5. **Four deviations: all ACKNOWLEDGED as intentional.** (a) The preflight pass with placeholder identities buys structural zero-serve refusals at roughly double lift cost; documented in §7.1; acceptable for 6d-a, and 6d-b's holder lanes should measure it. (b) Re-receiving the receiver's registry modules in restore is correct, since the restore resets code aliases and the old lowered bindings would otherwise dangle; it serves nothing and is covered by byte-equal relift on a two-module tree. (c) and (d) are harness-only.\n6. **Repeated-register ruling: RATIFIED, option (a).** The linearizer gives every value-producing instruction a fresh register, so `zero?` pins a real invariant and the agreement branch stays dormant until 6e's hand-built `[:call r f [x x]]` case.\n\n**Findings.** No P0, P1 or P2. Nothing blocks the merge.\n\n- **P3, design follow-up (deviation c).** Deviation (c) exists because a module-exported host function held in a window mid-application has no wire form unless the composition registers it in `:primitives`. For real compositions this means a lift refusal at every boundary between loading such an operator and calling it. The refusal is honest, but it is a hole in the any-pc invariant. Add a known-gaps row and give 6e or a later slice the task of lifting module exports by module coordinate via the existing host-export profiles.\n- **P3, docs.** The UCF status table drops the `:yin.k/not-quiescent` row, but the holder export in `holder/export.cljc` still raises it until 6d-b replaces the Quiesce step. Annotate the row as holder-only until 6d-b, or have 6d-b delete it.\n- **P3, docstring.** The `check-install!` docstring still says the carried child must be blocked; the code admits a running child at phase `:running`.\n\n**Wording amendments.** None are merge-blocking. After the merge: update the known-gaps row from \"acceptance pending\" to \"6d-a landed at d5987015; 6d-b and 6e pending\", and add the row for the module-export gap above. Add glm's one-sentence comment to the `zero?` pin in the layout test saying the per-group agreement clause is live only for non-projected vectors. Optionally normalise the \"amended 2026-10-16, owner invariant 2026-10-17\" citations to one timezone so the amendment does not appear to predate the invariant it cites.\n\n**Merge note.** origin/master has moved to 1d7a85a7 with two orchestrator-log commits that touch only `docs/orchestrator-log.md`, so the merge is conflict-free. Use a non-fast-forward merge so d5987015 stays the signed head rather than rebasing it to new hashes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md; git status --short; rg --files -g AGENTS.md; rg -n '\"'^### 3.7|''^### 3.8'\"' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md; rg -n '6d|module-export' docs/agents/known-gaps.md; rg -n 'zero\\\\?|check-install\"'!|not-quiescent'\"' test/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md; git status --short; rg --files -g AGENTS.md; rg -n '\"'^### 3.7|''^### 3.8'\"' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md; rg -n '6d|module-export' docs/agents/known-gaps.md; rg -n 'zero\\\\?|check-install\"'!|not-quiescent'\"' test/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n?? collab/1792172314000-engineer-6d-b.prompt.md\nAGENTS.md\n362:### 3.7 Amended document text\n468:### 3.8 Inventory and citation policy\n46:| the lift refuses a machine between instructions (`rows.cljc` / `lift-task*`) and a queued completed wake, the holder runs to a park (`driver.cljc` / `run-cycle`) and loses a computed boundary on tenure loss (`step-active`), the custody kind tables list three kinds, and the stack layout is undefined at jump pcs (`layout.cljc` / `stack-layouts`): the carrier cannot express, and the holder cannot pause at, an arbitrary pc | owner invariant 2026-10-17; Architect plan v2.1 `collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md` | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | 6d-a, 6d-b, 6e; acceptance pending | P1 |\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc:50:            (is (zero? @n) \"refused before any attachment\"))))))\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc:563:            (is (zero? @n) \"no stream was served\")\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc:566:              (is (zero? @n))\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc:586:            (is (zero? @n) \"refused before any attachment\"))))))\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc:635:          (is (zero? @n) \"refused before any attachment\")))))\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc:651:              (is (zero? @n) \"no stream was served\"))))))))\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc:716:                (is (zero? @n) \"refused before any attachment\"))\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc:733:                    (is (zero? @n) \"no stream was served\"))))\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc:813:          (is (zero? @repeated)))))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1962:(defn- check-install!\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:2025:    (run! #(check-install! recv %) installs)))\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1673:  (`:yin.k/not-quiescent` otherwise, §7.4.1) — a task with queued runnable\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2632:   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:3140:  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; queued work gives `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792170000000-architect-6da-fable-confirm.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log; sed -n '362,468p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md; sed -n '1955,1985p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '790,820p' test/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792170000000-architect-6da-fable-confirm.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log; sed -n '362,468p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md; sed -n '1955,1985p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '790,820p' test/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"6DA-FABLE-CONFIRM: SIGNED-OFF (head d5987015)\n\n**What I verified myself, not taken from the reviewers or engineers.** The staged diff file is byte-identical to `git diff b9103797..d5987015`. I read every source hunk in semantic, frame, layout, engine and rows, the full harness and test diffs, and the full docs diff. clj-kondo on all 13 changed Clojure files reports 0 errors, 0 warnings. I ran the JVM quick tier for the seven touched test namespaces in the worktree:\n\n| Run | Tests | Assertions | Failures |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| frame, layout, vm, rows, rows-custody, engine, safepoint-harness | 204 | 24,244 | 0 |\n\nThe printed measurements match the pinned tables. I did not rerun Node, Dart or the slow tier; for those I rely on the orchestrator's logged runs.\n\n**Per-point rulings**\n\n1. **Fidelity to plan v2.1: CONFIRMED.** The completion table, slots 17 and 18, the run row, the 19-slot task gate, ready-context frame judging with the descriptor passed through the task context, the factored `check-op-id-shape!` called from slot 18 with per-tag op-id indices, the census traversal that now skips only task slot 9 at all four sites, ready-row traversal in `lower-defect` including value-bearing completion fields, jump and terminator layouts, budget propagation through check-wait-set, poll-links, advance-install(s) via a run-child parameter, `scheduler-round-fuel` with at most one resume, `run-fuel` dispatching on control presence with one raw action and at most one bridge step and no `maybe-run` wrap, and `check-gate!` on vm-hot, step, run and run-fuel outside the loop via a private raw runner. All match §3.1, §3.2, §3.4 and the 6d-a change list. The §3.8 hygiene check passes: no normative clause assigns the guard to 6d-b, forbids completed link/install wakes, validates completed installs against live installs, restarts fuel through `maybe-run`, or promises one-cycle hold resolution.\n2. **Ledger golden: CONFIRMED by my own byte check.** Decoding each changed hex line, every differing byte lies inside a 64-character hex run immediately preceded by the CBOR text header and `segment` and `blake3-`. Nine digests change across journal frames 2, 9, 11, 13, 14 and 15, matching the inventory's entries. The projection line changes as a consequence. Classification `:address-only` stands.\n3. **Retained-FFI `:call-id` correction: CONFIRMED.** The completed-ffi-append test asserts the restored response wait carries the source wait's actual call id and that the request stream was not appended a second time. That is the right assertion target.\n4. **Flagged items: both CONFIRMED.** Row 20 measures exactly pcs 0, 1, 2 with obligations `#{s}`, independently pinned by the conservative-prefixes test, and the general rule in `check-returned-boundaries` is what governs. Row 17 is a like case: every excluded boundary carries exactly `#{host.mod/f}`, answered at the first measured boundary. The doc wording already uses the plural and the require-answered clause. cmd's caveat about the harness re-listing discharge's answer set is a real drift risk; carry it as the 6d-b note already drafted.\n5. **Four deviations: all ACKNOWLEDGED as intentional.** (a) The preflight pass with placeholder identities buys structural zero-serve refusals at roughly double lift cost; documented in §7.1; acceptable for 6d-a, and 6d-b's holder lanes should measure it. (b) Re-receiving the receiver's registry modules in restore is correct, since the restore resets code aliases and the old lowered bindings would otherwise dangle; it serves nothing and is covered by byte-equal relift on a two-module tree. (c) and (d) are harness-only.\n6. **Repeated-register ruling: RATIFIED, option (a).** The linearizer gives every value-producing instruction a fresh register, so `zero?` pins a real invariant and the agreement branch stays dormant until 6e's hand-built `[:call r f [x x]]` case.\n\n**Findings.** No P0, P1 or P2. Nothing blocks the merge.\n\n- **P3, design follow-up (deviation c).** Deviation (c) exists because a module-exported host function held in a window mid-application has no wire form unless the composition registers it in `:primitives`. For real compositions this means a lift refusal at every boundary between loading such an operator and calling it. The refusal is honest, but it is a hole in the any-pc invariant. Add a known-gaps row and give 6e or a later slice the task of lifting module exports by module coordinate via the existing host-export profiles.\n- **P3, docs.** The UCF status table drops the `:yin.k/not-quiescent` row, but the holder export in `holder/export.cljc` still raises it until 6d-b replaces the Quiesce step. Annotate the row as holder-only until 6d-b, or have 6d-b delete it.\n- **P3, docstring.** The `check-install!` docstring still says the carried child must be blocked; the code admits a running child at phase `:running`.\n\n**Wording amendments.** None are merge-blocking. After the merge: update the known-gaps row from \"acceptance pending\" to \"6d-a landed at d5987015; 6d-b and 6e pending\", and add the row for the module-export gap above. Add glm's one-sentence comment to the `zero?` pin in the layout test saying the per-group agreement clause is live only for non-projected vectors. Optionally normalise the \"amended 2026-10-16, owner invariant 2026-10-17\" citations to one timezone so the amendment does not appear to predate the invariant it cites.\n\n**Merge note.** origin/master has moved to 1d7a85a7 with two orchestrator-log commits that touch only `docs/orchestrator-log.md`, so the merge is conflict-free. Use a non-fast-forward merge so d5987015 stays the signed head rather than rebasing it to new hashes.\n### 3.7 Amended document text\n\nThe v2 amendment marker and unaffected replacements remain. The following are complete replacements for the affected clauses.\n\n#### 3.7.1 `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md`\n\n**Replace the proposed §2.3 ready-row entry and associated note:**\n\n> A ready row contains a delivery-state reference, completed-operation descriptor, outcome value, optional status, cursor-update row and optional raised-refusal value. Its delivery state is act or tail, never run. It is judged in context `:ready`, without an outstanding pending.\n>\n> Completed descriptors cover ordinary reads and writes, FFI response and request-append outcomes, link-request and link-response outcomes, and install outcomes. Link/install completion does not require the request transport or install entry that existed before completion. A successful link/install outcome may have no status. Try failures return their carried value; ordinary require failures restore `:link-refused` and refusal data for the terminal-resume path.\n>\n> Slot 9 is the sole cell census. A ready update pairs an existing canonical C with its independently emitted cell body. One C may name different bodies across the census and ordered update rows. Restore maps C through the census’s fresh-key map and applies the update body carried by that ready row.\n\n**Replace the proposed §3.3 frame-context sentence:**\n\n> Validate every task context first; then judge outstanding waits in `:wait`, active run states in `:running`, ready delivery states in `:ready`, parked states in `:parked`, and reified states in `:captured`. Ready descriptors receive completed-operation validation separately from pending validation. FFI call IDs are collected from outstanding waits and completed outcomes.\n\n**Replace the proposed §5.6 item 5:**\n\n> Check ordered region length and contents against `layout(p) ++ uses-in-order(p)`, with repeated-register agreement and the specified dead-prefix treatment. Check the non-tail and tail set equations separately. Ordered multiplicity is not the cardinality of `L(p)`. Retain the parked-window check.\n\n**Replace proposed §7.1 step 1:**\n\n> **Holds.** Refuse `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch` on an observe wait, held entry, unminted cursor, pending close or link request without its cursor. These are driver-owned incomplete transitions. Their completion can depend on external acknowledgement or backpressure; there is no one-cycle resolution guarantee. There is no ready-queue quiescence refusal: completed outcomes travel in order.\n\n**Replace proposed §7.1’s ready-walk clause and §7.2’s ready-restore clause:**\n\n> Each ready entry contributes its delivery state, completed descriptor, lifted outcome and optional refusal, and explicit resource-update bodies. Completed descriptors do not re-enter outstanding-pending validation.\n\n> Restore the ready queue in carried order using the task’s fresh resource keys. Restore each completion’s correlation and terminal-outcome fields. Nothing runs, polls or re-appends during lower. Fenced restoration retains the queue outside the scheduler, and the closed-gate guard prevents execution through every semantic execution entry.\n\n**Replace the proposed §7.3 retention clause:**\n\n> Stage-local execution returns the latest completed holder state explicitly on tenure failure. The ended holder retains that machine for inspection only. Recovery uses the last durable checkpoint; progress since it is lost. Inner immutable rebinding is not a channel to an outer catch.\n\n**Replace the proposed §7.4 fuel text:** use §3.4 above, including its honest guarantee verbatim.\n\n**Replace the proposed §5.7 driver/oracle text:** use §3.6 above, including the separate post-sweep fixture and ready outcome roots.\n\n**Change:** Propagates consensus P1-1/2/3, P2-4/7/8, ordered checks, coverage hygiene and amended hold wording into normative design text.\n\n#### 3.7.2 `yin.vm.semantic.md`\n\n**Replace the proposed §4.2 ready-row addition:**\n\n> The running row is `:run [segment-A pc env-cref window-cref k-cref]`. A completed outcome is `:ready [state-cref completion-cref value optional-status updates-cref optional-refusal]`; its act/tail delivery state has no outstanding pending. Completion descriptors and cursor updates follow the continuation design’s row grammar.\n\n**Append to the proposed §4.3 portability sentence:**\n\n> Receiver refusal checks include values waiting in completed outcomes, including nested values that become live only on resume.\n\n**Change:** Synchronizes the completed grammar and P2-7 oracle roots.\n\n#### 3.7.3 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`\n\n**Replace §7.7.4 step 1, located by “Quiesce: the ready-queue must be empty”:**\n\n> 1. **Retain the completed outcomes.** Enter the exporting gate and move the ordered ready queue into the retained export record. The live scheduler queue is empty. Preserve every outcome value, status, completion descriptor, cursor update and raised-refusal value. No queued operation is executed, discarded or reissued by this transition.\n\n**Replace v2’s proposed step-3 guard clause:**\n\n> The semantic execution entries refuse an exporting or ended machine before instruction, scheduler or bridge execution. This guard ships with running fenced restoration in 6d-a and remains outside the instruction loop.\n\n**Replace v2’s proposed “resolve in its next cycle” sentence:**\n\n> A task holding one of these incomplete driver transitions is not export-ready. Resolution may require repeated driver cycles, external acknowledgement or relief from backpressure; no fixed cycle count is promised.\n\n**Replace the proposed retained-boundary sentence:**\n\n> A holder whose tenure ends receives the latest completed state through stage-local failure handling, retains it for inspection, and offers nothing. Recovery remains the last durable checkpoint.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-9 closes the omitted Quiesce step; P1-2 and P2-8 synchronize fencing and retention; amended default 8 removes the false resolution promise.\n\n#### 3.7.4 `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`\n\n**Replace the proposed ready-row, validation and completed-write clauses:**\n\n> The row grammar includes run, ready and completed-operation rows as defined in the continuation design. Ready delivery frames are validated in context `:ready`; completion descriptors are validated independently of outstanding pendings.\n>\n> The task’s slot-9 row owns the cell census. Ready update rows refer to that census and carry their own cell bodies. Canonical traversal distinguishes census ownership from update references; one C may pair with different bodies across rows.\n>\n> Completed operation IDs, when present, receive shape-only validation in the slot-18 path of `check-pendings!`. Retained-write origin, occurrence and frontier checks remain in `check-op-id!`. Completed outcomes are excluded from retained operations, numbering and `protect!`; no completed operation is reissued by lower or resume.\n>\n> The canonical round-trip law still requires no execution between lower and relift.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P1-3 and P2-4/5; corrected context inventory.\n\n#### 3.7.5–3.7.6\n\nRetain v2’s replacements. References to completed wakes mean the grammar of v2.1 §3.1; no new pending-resource requirement is implied.\n\n#### 3.7.7 `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`\n\n**Replace the proposed I-9 entry:**\n\n> **I-9 — 2026-10-16, any-PC.** The current lift rejects active control and queued completed outcomes, while the holder runs unrestricted computation. Slices 6d-a, 6d-b and 6e extend the carrier to run states and complete scheduler products. 6d-a includes completed stream/FFI/link/install outcomes, cursor-update encoding, bounded bridge-aware fuel and closed-gate execution guards. 6d-b adds explicit state retention on tenure failure and holder migration from fuel boundaries. 6e extends backend layouts and transfer laws. Earlier static-safepoint-only lift prescriptions are historical. No compatibility promise is added.\n\n**Change:** Makes the revision entry describe the reconciled scope and sequencing.\n\n#### 3.7.8 Assessment and known gaps\n\nRetain v2’s supersession of the assessment. Locate implementation defects by symbol and quoted text rather than obsolete offsets. The gap closes only when its corresponding acceptance gates pass; writing v2.1 does not mark implementation complete.\n\n**Change:** Citation hygiene and accurate implementation status.\n\n### 3.8 Inventory and citation policy\n  \"The receiving kernel admits vector v (its loader's validation), under\n   address a when a is given.\"\n  [recv v a]\n  (try (let [r (module/attach-module recv v)] (or (nil? a) (contains? (:code-aliases r) a)))\n       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _e false)))\n\n\n(defn- check-install!\n  \"One install record: a phase the engine holds a carried child in, the\n   retained response link-install reads (status, manifest naming this\n   module, exports) with the image spawn-child loads, the response's link\n   id equal to the row's parent (the engine settles only the response of\n   its own link), the image the manifest names (continuation §11 Q3: the\n   manifest's :yin.semantic/code derivation record is the response's\n   :derivation, from the manifest's tree to the image's A), and a child\n   that is blocked: the engine links a halted child in the sweep it\n   halts in.\"\n  [recv inst]\n  (let [[mn phase parent response child] (:slots inst)\n        manifest (when (map? response) (:manifest response))\n        child-kind (nth (:slots child) 2)]\n    (when-not (contains? #{:running :parked} phase)\n      (outcome! :yin.k/undecodable :install-phase {:yin.k/name mn :yin.k/phase phase}))\n    (when-not (and (map? response) (= :ok (:status response)) (map? manifest)\n                   (not-any? #(= \"yin.admit\" (namespace %)) (filter keyword? (keys response)))\n                   (= mn (:yin.module/name manifest))\n                   (coll? (:yin.module/exports manifest)) (every? symbol? (:yin.module/exports manifest))\n                   (map? (:image response)) (attaches? recv (:value (:image response)) nil))\n      (outcome! :yin.k/undecodable :install-response {:yin.k/name mn}))\n    (when-not (and (some? parent) (= parent (:yin.link/id response)))\n      (outcome! :yin.k/undecodable :install-link-id {:yin.k/name mn}))\n                      (recur (vm/step m) (conj out [pc (:window m)])) out))\n              stk (loop [k stack out [] n 0]\n                    (when (> n 1000) (throw (ex-info \"Pure stack path did not halt\" {})))\n                    (if (vm/halted? k) out\n                        (let [[_ hp] (dvm/image-pc k (:pc k))\n                              pc (get (:h->a sg) hp)\n                              base (or (:stack-base (peek (:continuation k))) 0)]\n                          (recur (vm/step k)\n                                 (cond-> out (some? pc) (conj [pc (subvec (vec (:stack k)) base)]))\n                                 (inc n)))))\n              repeated (atom 0)]\n          (is (= (mapv first sem) (mapv first stk)))\n          (doseq [[[pc w] [_ region]] (map vector sem stk)]\n            (let [sequence (into (layout/stack-layout sg pc) (code/uses (nth (:vector sg) pc)))\n                  expected (mapv #(get w %) sequence)]\n              (is (= (count sequence) (count region)) (str \"pc \" pc))\n              (is (= (h/normalize expected) (h/normalize region)))\n              (doseq [[_ entries] (group-by first (map vector sequence region)) :when (< 1 (count entries))]\n                (swap! repeated inc)\n                (is (apply = (map second entries))))))\n          ;; Pinned as measured: linearize gives every operand a fresh\n          ;; register ((+ x x) is [:var 2 x] [:var 3 x] [:call 0 1 [2 3]]), so\n          ;; no projected program repeats one; a repeat would fail here loudly.\n          (is (zero? @repeated)))))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
