{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^### 3.[3789]|''^### 3.11|6d-b|2842|7.11.1|7.7.6|14.1'\"' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md; cat src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc; sed -n '1,240p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc; sed -n '1430,1515p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc; sed -n '2040,2310p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^### 3.[3789]|''^### 3.11|6d-b|2842|7.11.1|7.7.6|14.1'\"' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md; cat src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc; sed -n '1,240p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc; sed -n '1430,1515p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc; sed -n '2040,2310p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"15:2. **Fuel must reach children.** `scheduler-round` runs every install child with `vm/run` to a park or halt (`engine.cljc:1475`). `run-fuel` gives each task of the tree at most `n` instructions per program tick; a child stopped mid-body stays at phase `:running` (the arm already exists, `:1490-1491`) and is carried as a `:running` child task. The guarantee is cooperative: interruption between completed instructions, no wall-clock bound for one instruction or one host seam (`(apply f args)` at `semantic.cljc:193`, `vm/create-stream!` at `engine.cljc:480`, a bridge step, one sweep).\n16:3. **Tenure loss keeps the boundary, but adopts nothing.** `run-cycle` computes `m1` and then rechecks tenure before `m1` reaches the state (`driver.cljc:2120-2123`, `:2139`); the catch ends the run on the *pre-slice* state (`:2252-2253`). 6d-b assoc's `m1` before every later check so the ended holder retains the newest pure state as `:yin.k/retained-boundary` (inspection only). Expiry recovery stays the last durable checkpoint; **progress since it is lost**, the documents say so, and the mitigation is the composition's `hand-off` before the bound. Adoption of a retained boundary by a regrant is a ledger rule the owner may add later (decision 4); not in 6d.\n20:7. **Order**: 6d-a (grammar, validator, lift/lower, `run-fuel`, the jump-layout fix and the static checks, the reference-lane stepping harness) → then 6d-b (holder, custody, expiry, recovery, Stage E) **and** 6e (layouts' running mode, ordered stack check, `:push` canonicalisation, tail residuals, laws 4/5 on aligned boundaries) in parallel on two branches; docs with each slice. Nothing of 6d runs in parallel with module-pins' merge except writing the 6d-a brief.\n21:8. **Effort**: 6d-a 3 + 1.5 (3 rounds) = 4.5 days; 6d-b 2.5 + 1.5 = 4; 6e 3.5 + 1.5 = 5; docs 1.5 → **15 days**, up from v1's 11; no runtime measurement supports any wall-time forecast. Routing: codex (gpt-6.1-sol) implements, Fable reviews, glm/cmd as non-author fallback (owner 2026-10-15).\n22:9. **Owner decisions** (§3.10, defaults stated so 6d-a can start): new task slots 17/18 (recommended) vs. a mixed slot 13; fuel as a holder config key, default nil; explicit `hand-off` only, no auto-arm in 6d-b; no adoption of a retained boundary; keep edge-reachability liveness (Q6) and pin the pre-branch `ghost` refusal; completed `:put` wakes are completed operations, not retained writes; canonicalise `:push`; holds stay refusals as documented driver transients.\n23:10. **Readiness**: with the defaults, **6d-a is implementation-ready the moment module-pins commit 2 is on master**, and its brief can be written now. 6d-b and 6e are implementation-ready as specified here but depend on 6d-a's grammar. One acceptance item is a check, not an open design: whether the lane fragment contains a tail call with a non-empty ambient layout (6e item 6 settles it either way).\n101:Verified each cited site: `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:368-371` and `:403-408` (custody prose lists three kinds), `:433-435` (`frame/defect` contexts), `:1249-1252` (refusal rows naming the stepped-kernel refusal), `:2419-2425` (a second normative conformance text), `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:455-460` (kind rules), `:466-473` (custody table), `:797-801` (step 7 contexts), `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:800-805` (conformance runs \"to each safepoint\"), `:1912-1913` (\"no engine change\"), `:2842` (no-layer-collapsing row: hot loop untouched, lift over a blocked machine), `yin.vm.semantic.md:680-689` (explicit park is a no-wait safepoint; halt carries a result). v1 §3.3's safepoint definition (\"carries a delivery record and a pending wait\") was wrong for explicit park and halt; \"Every instruction boundary lifts\" overstated; the `:push` wording (\"no canonical state exists here\") was wrong (`stack.cljc:722-723` only advances pc); S1 vs P1 diagnostics conflicted. §3.7 is the corrected text; §3.8 the inventory, now at line granularity for every file `grep safepoint` reaches.\n207:### 3.3 Custody and lifecycle of a running root\n214:2. **Tenure dies after a completed slice** (at `:2123`, inside replay/emit/read, or at the next entry's `binding-check`). 6d-b restructures `run-cycle` so each stage's product is assoc'd into the state **before** the next tenure check (`(-> state (assoc :machine m1) require-tenure! …)`), and `step-active`'s catch ends the run on the state it was handed, which now carries the newest completed pure stage. `end-run` gates the machine `:ended` as today; the ended state additionally carries `:yin.k/retained-boundary {:yin.k/pc … :yin.k/segment … :yin.k/steps-since-checkpoint k}` for inspection and diagnostics. **No adoption**: an ended holder holds no tenure, so `exit-mint` refuses, `abort` refuses `:tenure-ended`/`:tenure-unconfirmed`, and the retained bytes are never offered or resumed. Why retention is safe: under the `:running` gate the slice performs no external observation (effects park; FFI requests are retained for the driver), so `m1` is pure local progress; retaining it loses nothing and discloses nothing.\n218:**Auto-arm before the bound** (decision 3): recommendation is **not in 6d-b**. `hand-off` stays the composition's explicit act (UCF §7.8); `:yin.k/at-boundary` is the signal. A later policy \"arm the exit when remaining tenure is below one renewal interval\" needs only `lease/holding?` and the clock the driver already reads (`:2060-2063`), no clock-to-instruction conversion, and can live in `compose` without touching the carrier.\n220:**Tests (6d-b)**: clock-controlled cuts (a) immediately before the slice, (b) immediately after it (the `:2123` check), (c) in replay, in emit and in the reader step, (d) between two slices at the next entry's `binding-check`, (e) during `exit-mint`/`prepare`/`offer` of a running root; in each: the source is fenced or ended, no effect was performed past the bound, and **either** a durably authorized successor exists (the offer was accepted under live tenure) **or** the recovery checkpoint is the last pack and `:yin.k/retained-boundary` reports the lost steps; byte-equality of `rehydrate-fenced` for a running root with waits and with a ready entry; two competing candidates for a `:running` offer; stale-source stepping refused by the gate guard; `abort` with uncertain offer refused; expired-grant admission refused.\n235:- `scheduler-round-fuel m n` = `check-wait-set` with `advance-installs` taking the budget: each install child is stepped with `(run-fuel child n)` instead of `(vm/run …)` (`engine.cljc:1475`); a child that returns with `:control` set is stored at phase `:running` (the existing `:else` arm, `:1490-1491`); a blocked child `:parked`; a halted child goes to `link-install` as today. Then `resume-from-run-queue` once. **Per-tree bound**: each task of the tree executes at most `n` instructions per program tick, so a tick is at most `n × (tasks in the tree)` instructions plus the sweep's scan plus the host seams listed below. Nested install children are tasks of the tree and get the same `n`.\n241:**Running install children in the carrier**: a child with `:control` set lifts as a `:running` child task (same walk); wire phase `:running` ↔ child kind `:running`; `check-install!` admits it (§3.1); receiver admission per child is unchanged (`check-modules!` after module-pins); a running child restores with its control set and continues in the next sweep; the de Bruijn backends refuse installs as today. Tests (6d-a item 8): a long initializer preempted mid-body, lifted, lowered, continued to `linked`; a nested initializer (child of a child); handoff while a child is running (6d-b).\n269:### 3.7 Amended document text (exact)\n277:> 9. **Preemptive migration at any pc is a primary invariant of `yin.vm`** (owner, 2026-10-17: \"preemptive migration, the ability to pause and continue at any PC, is a primary invariant of yin.vm. It is why yin.vm is defined as a continuation VM based on the CESK model. The UCF being only defined at a wait or parked state is a violation of this invariant\"). The carrier expresses the evaluator's complete task state between **any** two instructions, including the scheduler's products (remaining waits, completed wakes), not only its two delivery states; a safepoint is a boundary at which the program stops by itself. Slices 6d-a (carrier, reference lane), 6d-b (holder, custody, expiry) and 6e (backends) land it.\n349:**§8.3**, item 12: \"`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` from a kernel pc outside every body; a kernel stepped to a non-parking pc lifts as a `run` state (6e; until then the backend refusal is pinned as a limit); a `:push` pc relabels\". Add §8.6 (6d-a), §8.7 (6d-b), §8.8 (6e) = plan v2 §3.9.\n371:**§10 phase 6**: append \"*(Amended 2026-10-16: the phase-6 harness proved the delivery states only; slices 6d-a, 6d-b and 6e extend the gate to every instruction boundary and to the scheduler's products under the owner's any-pc invariant.)*\"\n393:**§7.7.6** (`:2161`) \"runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\" → \"runs to its next park, halt, or fuel boundary\".\n399:**§7.10** (`:2839`): \"Resume happens only at an instruction boundary the canonical vector defines (a parking transition, or the boundary a holder's fuel budget stopped at); the successor chain …\". **`:2842` No layer collapsing** → \"The engine's instruction loop is untouched: lifting is a pure function over the machine's value at any boundary, the one engine change is a gate check at the kernel's entry, and the exporting state is that value with its waits, queue and parked records moved into a retained record — driver discipline, not engine machinery.\"\n401:**§7.11** (`:2931-2942`): rename \"Continuation conformance harness\"; \"to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table — effect-producing calls included —\" → \"to **every boundary of the stepping driver** (continuation design §5.7), the complete §7.4.1 table's parking transitions included,\"; the cross-engine sentence \"one program at one aligned instruction boundary\". **§7.11.1** (`:3100-3130`): Invariant → \"At every instruction boundary, lifting and lowering preserve the next observable step (and, at a parking transition, its wait; with a non-empty queue, its order); a pc outside every body refuses.\" Setup: \"a nonsafepoint pc\" → \"a `:push` pc of the stack image (relabels) and a body-start pc (lifts)\"; expected: \"An undeclared pc gives `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; queued work gives `:yin.k/not-quiescent`\" → \"A pc outside every body gives `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; every pc of A lifts as a `run` state when the machine is between instructions; queued work travels as `:ready` rows and resumes in order\".\n413:#### 3.7.6 `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` §14.1\n415:Table 14.1.1 (`:3777-3788`): add `| Between instructions (:running) | Active control; ordered waits and completed wakes travel; the window is exactly L(pc); continue at pc. |`. `:3798` \"Static `:yin.safepoint/kinds` checks pc eligibility only\" → \"…checks a parking pc's kinds; a `run` pc is any pc of a body\". `:3827` → \"Validate the instruction boundary (§7.4.1 as amended); enter exporting before …\". `:3882` \"continue to the next safepoint\" → \"continue to the next park, halt or fuel boundary\". `:3900-3901` → \"A pc outside every body yields `not-at-safepoint`; queued work travels as `:ready` rows.\" `:4145` as UCF §7.8 step 9.\n419:New dated entry I-9: \"2026-10-16, any-pc. The lift refuses a machine between instructions (`rows.cljc:1656-1657`) and a non-empty ready queue (`:1652-1653`), the holder runs to a park (`driver.cljc:2120`), and every custody kind table lists three kinds, in violation of the owner's primary invariant (2026-10-17). Slices 6d-a, 6d-b and 6e add the `:run` and `:ready` rows, root kind `:running`, validator rules R1-R2 and the extended S1/C2/C3/P1, custody for running roots, fuel, expiry semantics, control-flow stack layouts and the harness at every boundary. Section 8's 'check the pc against the static safepoint set' (`:602`) and 'the safepoint harness must run each 7.4.1 row' (`:622`) describe the stack-shaped machine and are historical.\" No compatibility promise.\n425:> | the lift refuses a machine between instructions (`rows.cljc:1656-1657`) and a queued completed wake (`:1652-1653`), the holder runs to a park (`driver.cljc:2120`) and loses a computed boundary on tenure loss (`:2252-2253`), the custody kind tables list three kinds, and the stack layout is undefined at jump pcs (`layout.cljc:118-131`): the carrier cannot express, and the holder cannot pause at, an arbitrary pc | owner invariant 2026-10-17; Architect plan v2 `collab/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md` | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | 6d-a, 6d-b, 6e | P1 |\n427:### 3.8 Complete inventory of safepoint-only assertions\n453:| `:2842` | \"hot loop untouched … over the blocked machine's value\" | replace, §3.7.3 |\n454:| `:2931-2942`, `:3100-3130` | harness blocker; 7.11.1 | replace, §3.7.3 |\n455:| `:3186`, `:3252` | \"the safepoint row\"; \"The safepoint block adds obligations\" | keep (\"row\" names the 7.11.1 row; \"block\" the section) |\n494:| `yin.vm.linker.dht.md:3742`, `:3759`, `:3777-3798`, `:3827`, `:3882`, `:3900-3901`, `:4145` | 14.1 | §3.7.6 |\n510:| `driver.cljc:74-77`, `:318-321`, `:1119-1123`, `:2147-2154` | phase docstring; \"liftable safepoint (parked, blocked or halted)\"; `hand-off`; `at-safepoint?` | 6d-b: docstrings |\n511:| `export.cljc:274-282`, header | `kind-of` \"else :halted\"; `enter` queue refusal (`:107-114`, `:138`) | 6d-b: `:running` first; `first-refusal` deleted |\n512:| `compose.cljc:798-799`, `:842` | \"liftable safepoint, holding no custody\"; \"arms over the safepoint\" | 6d-b: \"at a park, halt or instruction boundary\" |\n513:| `ledger.cljc:277`; `authority/grant.cljc:74-75` | `#{:blocked :parked}` | 6d-b: add `:running` |\n516:| `semantic.cljc:2`, `:137`, `:331-333` | kernel docstring; `vm-hot` entry; `step`/`run` | 6d-a adds `run-fuel`; 6d-b adds the gate guard; docstring names both |\n517:| `engine.cljc:1458-1491` | `advance-install` \"`running` runs it until it halts or parks\" | 6d-a: budgeted; docstring |\n521:| `stage_e_scenarios.cljc:184-186` | \"no liftable safepoint, UCF 7.4.1\" | 6d-b: \"a hold, UCF 7.7.4\" |\n523:### 3.9 Slices, order, parallelism, effort\n532:                                              ├──►  6d-b (srvm-6d-b)   ┐  in parallel; both review-gated\n533:                                              └──►  6e   (srvm-6e)     ┘  independently; 6d-b merges first\n539:- 6d-b and 6e are independent of each other and both depend on 6d-a's grammar. The owner's invariant is **not** closed by 6d-a alone (fork-only); 6d-b closes it for the holder.\n548:- `engine.cljc`: `advance-install` takes a budget (`:1458-1491`); `scheduler-round-fuel` beside `scheduler-round` (`:2315-2326`).\n549:- `semantic.cljc`: public `run-fuel` (§3.4); no change to `vm-hot`'s loop (the gate guard is 6d-b).\n569:#### 6d-b: holder, custody, expiry, recovery\n578:7. Docs: §3.7.1 §7.3; §3.7.3 UCF §7.7.4, §7.7.6, §7.8, §7.10, §7.11, §7.11.1, `:2842`; §3.7.6.\n588:**Depends on.** 6d-a; independent of 6d-b. **Days.** 3.5 + 1.5 (3 rounds) = 5.\n595:| 6d-b holder, custody, expiry, recovery | 2.5 | 3 | 4 |\n608:3. **No automatic export at the bound in 6d-b**; `hand-off` stays explicit; `:yin.k/at-boundary` is the signal. A later \"arm when remaining tenure < one renewal interval\" policy belongs in `compose`, needs no VM clock. **Recommend as stated.**\n613:8. **Holds stay refusals** (`:observe`, held, unminted cursor, pending close, link cursor not installed), documented as driver seams mid-step that the driver resolves without guest execution; 6d-b verifies each resolves within one cycle and gives any that does not a known-gaps row. **Recommend as stated.**\n615:### 3.11 Risks\n637:**Implementation-ready**: 6d-a, as specified in §3.9, under the defaults of §3.10, **the moment module-pins commit 2 is on master**; its brief can be written now from §3.1, §3.2, §3.4, §3.5 (static part), §3.6, §3.9 and the §3.7 text it owns. 6d-b and 6e are specified to the same level and are ready to brief when 6d-a merges; they run in parallel. **Start first**: 6d-a. The owner's invariant is closed for the holder only when 6d-b lands; the plan says so and does not call it closed earlier.\n(ns yin.vm.ucf.holder.export\n  \"The exporting state of M-next D slice D8 (UCF 7.7.4; r3 1.8 and 1.9),\n   over the row carrier since phase 7b: the holder side of a lift, over a\n   machine value and data.\n\n   `enter` is the one-step transition into exporting: it sets\n   `:yin.k/gate :exporting` on the root, requires an empty ready queue,\n   and moves the wait set and every parked record into an export record.\n   The machine then has nothing to poll, resume or advance, and the gate\n   refuses what remains.  It refuses, as `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind\n   `:reason-mismatch`, a task that holds (root or any install child) an\n   `:observe` entry, any entry or cell carrying `:yin.k/held`, an\n   unminted cursor cell, a pending close, or a `:link-request` entry\n   whose response cursor is not installed.\n\n   `prepare` and `encode` are the split of r3 1.8, over `rows/lift-task`.\n   Prepare calls the exporter's `serve!` once per stream (recursively for\n   install children) and stores every answer in the record, keyed by\n   task path and resource id (the lift's `:serve-keyed`); `encode` is a\n   pure function of that record and the machine: it allocates and\n   publishes nothing, and the same record gives the same canonical pack\n   bytes.  The record retains the custody header and the composition's\n   source index the lift ran under: code travels by A, and every carried\n   A names its trees from that index (continuation §6.1).\n\n   `freeze` writes the export-recovery object, version 2: the published\n   pack (its address, its root id, its bytes) beside the holder-local\n   engine state the pack does not carry (gate, issue stamps, issued and\n   origin counters, per task) and the header, whose enrolled set never\n   travels.  The pack already carries the whole store, every parked\n   record and every module store the tasks reach, so no second body is\n   stored.  `rehydrate-fenced` restores it administratively through\n   `rows/lower-task {:fenced true}` and proves the published pack is\n   reproduced byte for byte.\n\n   `abort` returns the record to the machine only when the offer cannot\n   have been admitted (7.7.4, r3 1.9).\n\n   Nothing here knows a transport, a driver or a journal.\"\n  (:require [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint :as checkpoint]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Refusal data\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- non-portable\n  [data]\n  (assoc data\n         :yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable\n         :yin.k/kind :reason-mismatch))\n\n\n(defn- refused\n  [reason]\n  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/refused :yin.k/reason reason})\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The tree of machines: the root and every install child\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- machines\n  \"`[path machine]` of `vm` and, recursively, each install child, in\n   install-name order of the map's own iteration.\"\n  [path vm]\n  (cons [path vm]\n        (mapcat (fn [[m inst]]\n                  (when (map? (:vm inst))\n                    (machines (conj path m) (:vm inst))))\n                (:installs vm))))\n\n\n(defn- held-entry?\n  [x]\n  (and (map? x) (contains? x :yin.k/held)))\n\n\n(defn- refusal-at\n  \"The first thing machine `m` holds that has no wire form, as the\n   data the refusal carries, or nil.\"\n  [m]\n  (let [waits (:wait-set m)\n        cells (filter map? (vals (:resources m)))]\n    (cond\n      (some #(= :observe (:reason %)) waits)\n      {:yin.k/hold :observe}\n\n      (or (some held-entry? waits) (some held-entry? cells))\n      {:yin.k/hold :held}\n\n      (some :yin.k/unminted cells)\n      {:yin.k/hold :unminted-cursor}\n\n      (seq (:yin.k/closes m))\n      {:yin.k/hold :pending-close}\n\n      (some #(and (= :link-request (:reason %)) (not (contains? % :cursor)))\n            waits)\n      {:yin.k/hold :link-cursor-not-installed})))\n\n\n(defn- first-refusal\n  [vm]\n  (some (fn [[path m]]\n          (when (seq (:ready-queue m))\n            {:yin.k/status :yin.k/not-quiescent\n             :yin.k/path path\n             :yin.k/ready (count (:ready-queue m))}))\n        (machines [] vm)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Entering exporting\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn enter\n  \"Enter exporting from `machine`.  Answers\n   `{:status :ok :machine m :record r}`: `m` is `machine` with the gate\n   `:exporting`, an empty wait set and no parked record, and `r` the\n   export record, holding the wait set in order, every parked record\n   (the row carrier carries them all, continuation §2.5), and the gate\n   to return to.  Otherwise answers the data outcome of the first\n   refusal, leaving `machine` as it was: `:yin.k/not-quiescent` for\n   queued work in the root or a child, a `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind\n   `:reason-mismatch` (naming the hold and the task path), or\n   `:yin.k/refused` for a machine that is already exporting or ended.\"\n  [machine]\n  (cond\n    (contains? #{:exporting :ended} (vm/gate-mode machine))\n    (refused :not-running)\n\n    :else\n    (or (first-refusal machine)\n        (some (fn [[path m]]\n                (when-some [h (refusal-at m)]\n                  (non-portable (assoc h :yin.k/path path))))\n              (machines [] machine))\n        {:status :ok\n         :machine (cond-> (assoc machine :yin.k/gate :exporting :wait-set [])\n                    (contains? machine :parked) (assoc :parked {}))\n         :record {:wait-set (vec (:wait-set machine))\n                  :parked (:parked machine)\n                  :gate (vm/gate-mode machine)\n                  :served {}}})))\n\n\n(defn- reinstated\n  \"`machine`, an exporting machine, carrying `record`'s waits and\n   parked records again: the value the lift reads.\"\n  [machine record]\n  (-> (reduce-kv (fn [restored module-name child-record]\n                   (update-in restored [:installs module-name :vm]\n                              reinstated child-record))\n                 machine (or (:children record) {}))\n      (assoc :wait-set (:wait-set record))\n      (update :parked merge (:parked record))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Prepare, then encode\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- check-header!\n  \"A header is nil (the fork lift) or a map carrying the arbitration,\n   the counter and the enrolled set; anything else is a defect of the\n   caller, not a refusal.  The lift judges the rest.\"\n  [header]\n  (when (some? header)\n    (when-not (and (map? header)\n                   (contains? header :yin.k/arbitration)\n                   (contains? header :yin.k/next-op-seq)\n                   (set? (:yin.k/enrolled header)))\n      (throw (ex-info \"Prepare of a malformed header\"\n                      {:yin.k/hint :malformed-header})))))\n\n\n(defn- lift\n  \"rows/lift-task of the record over `machine`, under the record's header\n   and source index, every stream asked of `keyed`: `{:status :ok :wire\n   w}` or the lift's refusal as data. A throw without the refusal\n   vocabulary is a defect and propagates.\"\n  [machine record keyed]\n  (try\n    {:status :ok\n     :wire (rows/lift-task (reinstated machine record) nil\n                           {:header (:header record)\n                            :source-index (:source-index record)\n                            :serve-keyed keyed})}\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n      (let [d (ex-data e)]\n        (if (and (map? d) (contains? d :yin.k/status)) d (throw e))))))\n\n\n(defn- seeded-by-handle\n  \"handle -> descriptor for every key the retained `table` already\n   answers, resolved through `machine`'s own resource bindings at each\n   key's task path: the alias reuse an interrupted prepare had already\n   proved, reconstructed before any new key is served, so a retry never\n   serves a handle the record already answers under another alias.\n   Handles are never persisted; this mapping lives within the one\n   call.\"\n  [machine table]\n  (let [at (into {} (machines [] machine))]\n    (into {}\n          (keep (fn [[[path id] descriptor]]\n                  (when-some [h (get-in (get at path) [:resources id])]\n                    [h descriptor])))\n          table)))\n\n\n(defn prepare\n  \"Prepare `record`: call `serve!` once per stream of the exporting\n   `machine`, install children included, and answer\n   `{:status :ok :record r}` with every answer retained in `r`, or the\n   data outcome of the lift's refusal with `:record` added, carrying\n   what was served before the refusal so a retry never serves a stream\n   twice -- not even under a resource alias the refused attempt never\n   reached, whose handle the retained table and the machine's bindings\n   still identify.  The row lift serves while it walks, so a refusal it\n   decides after the walk (`:op-seq-exhausted`, `:unprotected-pending`,\n   an op id on an unenrolled stream, the custody grammar) still finds\n   the streams it reached served and retained (phase-7a D4).\n\n   The served table is keyed by `[task-path resource-id]` and holds the\n   descriptor `serve!` answered. This table is plain data, but the live\n   waits can contain authentic values: `freeze` is the persistence seam.\n   A handle is asked at most once, across the calls of one retained\n   record, even when two resources hold it.  `header` is nil for the\n   fork lift, or the exclusive custody header (`rows/lift-task`'s\n   `:header`), kept in the record as `:header`; `source-index` is the\n   composition's `{A #{tree-address}}` (continuation §6.1), kept as\n   `:source-index`.  Prepare mints nothing: the occurrence was minted by\n   the driver before it was called.\"\n  [machine record serve! header source-index]\n  (check-header! header)\n  (let [table (atom (:served record))\n        by-handle (atom (seeded-by-handle machine (:served record)))\n        once (fn [k h]\n               (let [t @table]\n                 (cond\n                   (contains? t k)\n                   (let [served (get t k)]\n                     ;; an alias of an already-served key asks again for\n                     ;; nothing: the handle is known served from here on\n                     (swap! by-handle assoc h served)\n                     served)\n                   (contains? @by-handle h)\n                   (let [served (get @by-handle h)]\n                     ;; a handle already served under another resource id\n                     ;; is the one stream: no second call, and the table\n                     ;; must hold this key too or the record is unprepared\n                     ;; for a resource it reached\n                     (swap! table assoc k served)\n                     served)\n                   :else\n                   (let [served (serve! h)]\n                     ;; a refusal is not an answer: a retry asks again\n                     (when (some? served)\n                       (swap! table assoc k served)\n                       (swap! by-handle assoc h served))\n                     served))))\n        record (assoc record :header header :source-index source-index)\n        r (lift machine record once)]\n    (if (= :ok (:status r))\n      {:status :ok :record (assoc record :served @table)}\n      (assoc r :record (assoc record :served @table)))))\n\n\n(defn- kind-of\n  \"The lifted task's kind, by the lift's own rule.\"\n  [m]\n  (let [value (:value m)]\n    (cond\n      (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value))\n           (contains? (:parked m) (:id value))) :parked\n      (seq (:wait-set m)) :blocked\n      :else :halted)))\n\n\n(defn encode\n  \"The lift of the prepared `record` over `machine`, from a `serve-keyed`\n   that only reads the record, under the header and source index the\n   record retains: `{:status :ok :bytes b :address a :root B :kind k}`,\n   `b` the pack's canonical bytes, `a` the pack address (BLAKE3 of `b`,\n   the storage key), `B` the root id inside it; or the lift's refusal as\n   data.  It allocates and publishes nothing, and one record gives equal\n   bytes every time.  A stream the record has no answer for is a defect\n   of the caller (an unprepared record), not a refusal.\"\n  [machine record]\n  (let [served (:served record)\n        r (lift machine record\n                (fn [k _h]\n                  (if (contains? served k)\n                    (get served k)\n                    (throw (ex-info \"Encode of an unprepared record\"\n                                    {:yin.k/hint :unprepared-stream})))))]\n    (if (= :ok (:status r))\n      (let [wire (:wire r)]\n        {:status :ok\n         :bytes (cbor/encode wire)\n         :address (rows/pack-address wire)\n         :root (:yin.k/root wire)\n         :kind (kind-of (reinstated machine record))})\n      r)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Freeze: the export-recovery object, version 2\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- recovery-address\n  [bytes]\n  (let [algorithm jing/default-hash-algorithm]\n    (keyword \"segment\"\n             (str (get-in jing/registry [algorithm :address-id]) \"-\"\n                  (jing/digest-bytes algorithm bytes)))))\n\n\n(defn- recovery-metadata\n  \"The holder-local engine state of one task the pack does not carry:\n   the gate to return to, each wait's issue stamp in wait order, the\n   issued and origin counters, and each install child's, recursively.\"\n  [machine record]\n  {:gate (:gate record)\n   :origins (:origins machine)\n   :issues (mapv :yin.k/issue (:wait-set record))\n   :issued (:yin.k/issued machine)\n   :children (into {}\n                   (map (fn [[module-name install]]\n                          [module-name\n                           (recovery-metadata\n                             (:vm install)\n                             (or (get-in record [:children module-name])\n                                 {:gate (vm/gate-mode (:vm install))\n                                  :wait-set (:wait-set (:vm install))}))]))\n                   (:installs machine))})\n\n\n(defn freeze\n  \"The export-recovery object of the prepared record, version 2:\n\n     {:yin.k/export-recovery true\n      :yin.k/version         2\n      :yin.k/pack-address    a        ; the published pack's address\n      :yin.k/root            B        ; the root id inside it\n      :yin.k/pack-bytes      base64   ; the pack, byte for byte\n      :yin.k/header          h        ; nil for a fork; the enrolled set\n                                      ; stays here, never in the pack\n      :yin.k/state           {:gate :origins :issued :issues :children}}\n\n   Answers `{:status :ok :bytes rb :address ra :pack-bytes b\n   :pack-address a :root B :kind k}`, `rb` the recovery object's\n   canonical bytes and `ra` their BLAKE3 address, or the encode's\n   refusal.  Both objects must be stored durably before a fence is\n   recorded.\"\n  [machine prepared-record]\n  (let [e (encode machine prepared-record)]\n    (if (not= :ok (:status e))\n      e\n      (let [recovery {:yin.k/export-recovery true\n                      :yin.k/version 2\n                      :yin.k/pack-address (:address e)\n                      :yin.k/root (:root e)\n                      :yin.k/pack-bytes (jing/bytes->base64 (:bytes e))\n                      :yin.k/header (:header prepared-record)\n                      :yin.k/state (recovery-metadata machine prepared-record)}\n            bytes (cbor/encode recovery)]\n        {:status :ok :bytes bytes :address (recovery-address bytes)\n         :pack-bytes (:bytes e) :pack-address (:address e)\n         :root (:root e) :kind (:kind e)}))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Rehydrate: the fenced recovery\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- invalid!\n  [data]\n  (throw (ex-info \"Invalid export recovery\" (merge {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable} data))))\n\n\n(defn- header-agrees?\n  \"The recovery's header is the custody the pack's root slot carries:\n   nil exactly for a fork; otherwise the slot's keys by kind (blocked or\n   parked: occurrence, arbitration, counter and origin; halted: origin),\n   a nil header value being an absent key, as the lift writes it.\"\n  [header baseline]\n  (if (nil? header)\n    (true? (:yin.k/fork baseline))\n    (let [ks (if (= :halted (:yin.k/kind baseline))\n               [:yin.k/origin]\n               [:yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq :yin.k/origin])]\n      (and (not (:yin.k/fork baseline))\n           (= (into {} (filter (comp some? val)) (select-keys header ks))\n              (select-keys baseline ks))))))\n\n\n(defn- counter?\n  [x]\n  (or (nil? x) (and (integer? x) (<= 0 x))))\n\n\n(defn- valid-state?\n  \"The recovery state fits the decoded task: one issue stamp per carried\n   wait, and one child state per carried install, recursively.\"\n  [task state]\n  (let [ss (:slots task)\n        installs (nth ss 12)\n        waits (nth ss 13)]\n    (and (map? state)\n         (contains? #{nil :running :exporting :ended} (:gate state))\n         (vector? (:issues state))\n         (= (count (:issues state)) (count waits))\n         (every? counter? (:issues state))\n         (counter? (:issued state))\n         (counter? (:origins state))\n         (map? (:children state))\n         (= (set (keys (:children state))) (into #{} (map #(first (:slots %))) installs))\n         (every? (fn [inst]\n                   (let [[mn _ _ _ child] (:slots inst)]\n                     (valid-state? child (get-in state [:children mn]))))\n                 installs))))\n\n\n(defn- carried-trees\n  \"{A #{tree}} every task of the decoded tree carries: the composition's\n   index as the prepare that published the pack saw it, restricted to the\n   carried segments, which is all a relift of the same tree reads.\"\n  [task]\n  (let [ss (:slots task)]\n    (reduce (fn [acc inst] (merge acc (carried-trees (nth (:slots inst) 4))))\n            (or (:yin.k/trees (nth ss 10)) {})\n            (nth ss 12))))\n\n\n(defn- extract-recovered\n  \"The fenced machine and its export record from the lower's fenced\n   restore of one task: the waits and parked records the lower kept out\n   of the scheduler move into the record, the waits restamped with the\n   recovered issues; the holder-local counters come from `state`.\"\n  [machine state]\n  (let [children (into {}\n                       (map (fn [[module-name install]]\n                              [module-name\n                               (extract-recovered (:vm install)\n                                                  (get-in state [:children module-name]))]))\n                       (:installs machine))\n        waits (mapv (fn [entry issue]\n                      (if (some? issue)\n                        (assoc entry :yin.k/issue issue)\n                        (dissoc entry :yin.k/issue)))\n                    (:yin.k/recovery-waits machine)\n                    (:issues state))\n        record {:wait-set waits\n                :parked (or (:yin.k/recovery-parked machine) {})\n                :recovered? true\n                :gate (:gate state)\n                :served {}\n                :children (into {} (map (fn [[module-name recovered]]\n                                          [module-name (:record recovered)]))\n                                children)}\n        fenced (reduce-kv (fn [parent module-name recovered]\n                            (assoc-in parent [:installs module-name :vm]\n                                      (:machine recovered)))\n                          (cond-> (-> machine\n                                      (dissoc :yin.k/recovery-waits :yin.k/recovery-parked\n                                              :yin.k/custody :yin.k/closes :yin.k/issued :origins)\n                                      (assoc :wait-set [] :parked {} :yin.k/gate :exporting))\n                            (some? (:origins state)) (assoc :origins (:origins state))\n                            (some? (:issued state)) (assoc :yin.k/issued (:issued state)))\n                          children)]\n    {:machine fenced :record record}))\n\n\n(defn rehydrate-fenced\n  \"Restore a version-2 export-recovery object into fresh receiver-owned\n   values (UCF 7.7.4).  Before any attachment: the object's tag and\n   version (`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`), its bytes against `address`\n   (`:yin.k/hash-mismatch`), the pack through the rows inspector (its\n   address against its bytes, the carrier, the custody grammar), the pack\n   root against the object's, the header against the root's custody\n   slot, and the state against the carried tree (any of these\n   `:yin.k/undecodable`, or the inspector's own refusal).  Then\n   `rows/lower-task {:fenced true}` over `receiver`, `attach` once per\n   stream identity: every task stays exporting, its waits and parked\n   records outside the scheduler and in the record, its id counter\n   exact; no program code runs, no stream is read, written, minted or\n   closed, no install child initializes.  Never restores tenure,\n   custody, scheduler waits or a runnable gate.  Last, the recovered\n   record is prepared again from what was attached and must encode the\n   published pack byte for byte, or the answer is `:yin.k/undecodable`\n   with `:yin.k/reason :recovery-inconsistent`.  Answers `{:status :ok\n   :machine m :record r}`, `r` marked `:recovered?` (abort refuses it).\n   opts :yin.k/admitted-modules is the receiver composition's name/address map\n   for carried install children, passed to the lower (default empty).\"\n  [receiver recovery-bytes {:keys [address attach] :as opts}]\n  (try\n    (let [recovery (cbor/decode recovery-bytes)]\n      (cond\n        (not (and (map? recovery)\n                  (true? (:yin.k/export-recovery recovery))\n                  (integer? (:yin.k/version recovery))\n                  (= 2 (:yin.k/version recovery))))\n        {:yin.k/status :yin.k/profile-mismatch}\n\n        (not (jing/segment-bytes-match? address recovery-bytes))\n        {:yin.k/status :yin.k/hash-mismatch}\n\n        :else\n        (let [pack-bytes (jing/base64->bytes (:yin.k/pack-bytes recovery))\n              header (:yin.k/header recovery)\n              state (:yin.k/state recovery)\n              baseline (checkpoint/inspect (:yin.k/pack-address recovery) pack-bytes)\n              _ (when (:yin.k/status baseline) (invalid! baseline))\n              wire (cbor/decode pack-bytes)\n              task (rows/decode wire)\n              _ (when-not (= (:yin.k/root recovery) (:yin.k/root baseline))\n                  (invalid! {:yin.k/reason :root}))\n              _ (check-header! header)\n              _ (when-not (header-agrees? header baseline)\n                  (invalid! {:yin.k/reason :header}))\n              _ (when-not (valid-state? task state)\n                  (invalid! {:yin.k/reason :state}))\n              attached (atom {})\n              restored (rows/lower-task\n                         receiver wire\n                         (fn [descriptor]\n                           (let [identity (:dao.stream/identity descriptor)]\n                             (if (contains? @attached identity)\n                               (:answer (get @attached identity))\n                               (let [answer (attach descriptor)]\n                                 (swap! attached assoc identity {:answer answer :descriptor descriptor})\n                                 answer))))\n                         (assoc (select-keys opts [:yin.k/admitted-modules]) :fenced true))\n              {:keys [machine record]} (extract-recovered restored state)\n              ;; each attached handle answers the descriptor it was served under\n              by-handle (into {}\n                              (map (fn [{:keys [answer descriptor]}]\n                                     [(:dao.stream/handle answer)\n                                      (select-keys descriptor [:dao.stream/identity\n                                                               :dao.stream/channel])]))\n                              (vals @attached))\n              prepared (prepare machine record #(get by-handle %) header (carried-trees task))\n              reproduced (when (= :ok (:status prepared))\n                           (encode machine (:record prepared)))]\n          (if (and (= :ok (:status reproduced))\n                   (= (vec pack-bytes) (vec (:bytes reproduced))))\n            {:status :ok :machine machine :record (assoc (:record prepared) :recovered? true)}\n            {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable\n             :yin.k/reason :recovery-inconsistent}))))\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) failure\n      (or (when (:yin.k/status (ex-data failure)) (ex-data failure))\n          {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable}))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Abort\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- not-appended?\n  \"True when `outcome`, the answer of an offer attempt's append, proves\n   the value was not appended.  `dao.stream`'s closed append outcome\n   set is {ok full invalid-value closed refused transport-error}: the\n   four refusals prove it; `ok` proves the opposite, and\n   `transport-error` stays the unknown-delivery refusal, as does no\n   answer.\"\n  [outcome]\n  (contains? #{:dao.stream/full :dao.stream/invalid-value\n               :dao.stream/closed :dao.stream/refused}\n             outcome))\n\n\n(defn abort\n  \"Return the exporting `machine` to local execution from `record`.\n\n   `attempts` is the offer-attempt history the composition persisted:\n   one map per attempt whose intent was durable before the send, with\n   `:append` the outcome its append answered (absent when none did).\n   Abort is legal when there is no attempt, or when every attempt's\n   append proves the value was not appended; anything else, including\n   an authority's refusal of one attempt, leaves the source fenced.  A\n   recovered record (`rehydrate-fenced`) is refused `:restarted`: a\n   restart never hands execution back.\n\n   A machine under custody (`:yin.k/custody`) is a holder exporting a\n   successor: it may abort only with `tenure`, `{:now n :bound b :live\n   true}`: `n` before `b` in one unit, and `:live` the ledger reader's\n   evidence that this lease is the occurrence's active lease at its\n   epoch.  A clock reading alone is not tenure.  After the lease has\n   ended its old local machine is never restored.\n\n   Answers `{:status :ok :machine m}` or `:yin.k/refused` data with\n   reason `:restarted`, `:not-exporting`, `:tenure-ended` (at or past\n   the bound), `:tenure-unconfirmed` (no ledger evidence) or\n   `:offer-possibly-accepted`.\"\n  [machine record attempts tenure]\n  (cond\n    (:recovered? record)\n    (refused :restarted)\n\n    (not= :exporting (vm/gate-mode machine))\n    (refused :not-exporting)\n\n    (and (contains? machine :yin.k/custody)\n         (some? (:now tenure))\n         (some? (:bound tenure))\n         (>= (:now tenure) (:bound tenure)))\n    (refused :tenure-ended)\n\n    (and (contains? machine :yin.k/custody)\n         (not (and (some? (:now tenure))\n                   (some? (:bound tenure))\n                   (true? (:live tenure)))))\n    (refused :tenure-unconfirmed)\n\n    (not (every? (comp not-appended? :append) attempts))\n    (refused :offer-possibly-accepted)\n\n    :else\n    (let [m (reinstated machine record)\n          prior (:gate record)]\n      {:status :ok\n       :machine (if (some? prior)\n                  (assoc m :yin.k/gate prior)\n                  (dissoc m :yin.k/gate))})))\n(ns yin.vm.ucf.holder.driver\n  \"The custody driver of M-next D slices D13 and D14 (UCF 7.7.2 to\n   7.7.8, 7.8, 7.9; r3 1.5, 1.6, 1.7, 1.8, 1.9 and 1.10; the D9 header\n   and D10 lower-inputs rulings; linker-dht 14.2.2): one explicit\n   composition-local step state, stepped by the composition, that\n   carries a candidate from bytes to a task under custody, drives that\n   task's run, and -- D14 -- carries the source half (a first export\n   and its offer) and the exit half (the successor, the report, the\n   release and the closure) over a write-ahead progress journal.\n\n   The driver reaches the authority through exactly the three remote\n   paths of r3 1.5, all composition-supplied functions: the front's\n   request stream (`append-request!`), the version-1 positional\n   `reply-inbox` and `outcome-inbox`, and the authenticated ledger\n   reader (`read-ledger!`, whose attributed records D3's\n   `read-evidence` and `custody/binding-evidence` fold).  The lease\n   clock (`clock`) is the composition's too, and dao.lease's own holder\n   discipline -- `observe-grant`, `holding?`, `due-to-renew?`,\n   `observe-renewal`, `stop` -- is the tenure arithmetic over it.\n\n   The phases, one `:phase` key:\n\n   :validating -- fetch and validate with zero side effects: the rows\n   inspector over the bytes and address (`rows.checkpoint/inspect`), then\n   `rows/lower-task` under `:exclusive` with no grant, which refuses\n   before any attachment, so nothing is attached and no machine is\n   exposed: it answers `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` for a valid exclusive\n   blocked or parked root; anything else is a data refusal with no\n   cleanup owed.  A halted exclusive root is not a lease subject: it\n   lowers once, gated `:ended`, and the driver publishes it with no\n   custody.\n\n   :proposing -- check the authority attachment (a missing one is\n   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the arbitration), propose through the\n   front with a fresh proposal id, and observe the grant from the\n   ledger: while the proposal is unanswered the step answers\n   `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`; a grant to another, or an occurrence that\n   can never be granted again, answers `:yin.k/not-holder` with the\n   lease state observed and releases nothing.  A grant to this driver\n   is accepted only whole: `custody/binding-evidence` over the same\n   records (an invalid binding -- none, a duplicate, an inexact epoch,\n   one outside the grant's transaction, one that mismatches its grant\n   -- is `:yin.k/not-holder` followed by a release), then D3's\n   `read-evidence` (unavailable history is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, never\n   an empty prefix, and never a release: custody was not accepted),\n   then the admitted-variant rule (the address must be among the fold's\n   admitted variants of the granted occurrence -- equality of supplied\n   addresses proves nothing), then current tenure: the fold's live\n   lease at the binding's epoch and dao.lease's bound, rechecked before\n   any execution or IO is scheduled.  Only then D10's lower runs, with\n   the checkpoint address, the composition's protection declaration and\n   the grant evidence; a lower failure is after the grant, and releases\n   first.  A regrant replays from the checkpoint: the lower starts\n   input at zero under the evidence's prefix.\n\n   :activating -- an authenticated grant is retained with its original\n   observation basis, without a machine. Control ticks may renew it;\n   only a program tick revalidates and lowers it.\n\n   :running -- run under the gate and drive the writer's and reader's\n   steps: tenure rechecked, the renewal sent through D2's\n   `:yin.k/renewal` request before half the duration and never past the\n   bound, the machine's internal computation, replay below the\n   frontier, `writer/emit`, `reader/step`, and every attributed record\n   the readers answer routed to the writer's `discharge`, the reader's\n   `settle` or this driver's own control arm.  All IO stops at the\n   bound; a run end (:stale, :intent-conflict, :input-conflict, a\n   replay divergence, the bound) gates the machine `:ended`, appends\n   one diagnostic, and releases -- cleanup, not recovery: a release\n   never clears a quarantine, never completes without accepted\n   completion evidence, and the occurrence of an ordinary failed run\n   stays regrantable under the ordinary policy.\n\n   :safepoint -- the machine has nothing left to drive (halted, no wait\n   entry, no queued work); the exit half (D14) owns what follows, under\n   tenure that is still held: renewal and the recheck continue while it\n   runs, and all IO stops at the bound.\n\n   :releasing -- a pending release is retried with the identical\n   request until authenticated carriage is acknowledged, then its failure is\n   the driver's answer.  :failed is terminal.\n\n   The D14 phases and entries beside these:\n\n   :exporting -- the source half over a machine the composition hands\n   the driver (`source`, below): enter exporting, mint the occurrence\n   once and journal it before prepare (the D9 ruling), prepare, encode,\n   put the export-recovery object and the pack bytes in the content store,\n   journal the fenced record, then the bracketed offer -- an intent\n   record durable before each send, an attempt record after it, an\n   acknowledgment once the front's authenticated reply admits the offer.\n   A nil :arbitration in the config is the composition's fork decision:\n   the row lift carries no custody, nothing is minted, offered or\n   journaled, and the driver answers the bytes (:lifted).\n\n   :exiting -- the exit half of a restarted holder, journaled but\n   machine-less: it reconciles committed reports, carries release and\n   watches for closure. An uncommitted report returns to candidacy,\n   without borrowing old tenure. A live driver runs the exit body from\n   :safepoint under its own tenure.\n\n   :exited -- the exit completed: the report committed, the release\n   carried, the closure observed and, for a continuation, the\n   successor's offer admitted.  :lifted and :aborted are the fork's and\n   a legal abort's terminals.  :stalled is an uncertain journal append:\n   everything stops -- program steps and sends alike -- until the\n   composition reopens the journal and reconciles through `reopen`.\n\n   The source is a candidate like any other: the driver holds bytes,\n   never the source's local machine, so the source itself resumes only\n   through a grant to itself, never on data.  No transport is known\n   here; the machine's own resource handles are the only ones touched,\n   by the writer and the reader.\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [dao.lease :as lease]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.authority.completion :as completion]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.authority.front :as front]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.custody :as custody]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.holder.evidence :as evidence]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.holder.export :as export]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.holder.inbox :as inbox]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.holder.reader :as reader]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.holder.writer :as writer]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.ledger :as ledger]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint :as checkpoint]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Assembly\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private classes\n  \"The three protection classes of r3 1.11.\"\n  #{:enrolled :at-least-once :fail-stop})\n\n\n(def ^:private seams\n  \"The composition-supplied functions the driver steps through.\"\n  [:append-request! :read-ledger!\n   :clock :observe! :append-diagnostic! :attach! :serve! :source-index])\n\n\n(def ^:private optional-seams\n  \"Composition-supplied functions the driver uses only when asked.\"\n  [:enroll!])\n\n\n(defn- check!\n  [ok what data]\n  (when-not ok\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"The candidate driver needs \" what)\n                    (into {} (cons {:yin.k/hint :assembly} data))))))\n\n\n(defn- check-interval!\n  \"The renewal interval's own shape gate, as dao.lease's holder runs it\n   at assembly: a duration, in a unit of the table, within the per-unit\n   bound.  The sizing against the granted duration -- strictly below\n   half -- is checked where the two are known together, at the grant.\"\n  [units interval]\n  (check! (lease/duration? interval)\n          \"a renewal interval: a single-entry {unit positive-integer} map\"\n          {:renewal-interval interval})\n  (let [[unit magnitude] (first interval)]\n    (check! (some? (get units unit))\n            \"a renewal interval whose unit is in the unit table\"\n            {:renewal-interval interval :units units})\n    (check! (<= magnitude (quot lease/magnitude-limit (get units unit)))\n            \"a renewal interval within the per-unit bound\"\n            {:renewal-interval interval})))\n\n\n(defn- check-common!\n  \"The checks every driver constructor runs: the identity, the\n   protection declaration, the receiving machine, the renewal interval,\n   the fn seams, the progress journal, the content store and the carrier\n   medium -- the D14 seams beside the D13 ones.  A candidate that never\n   exits still carries them: a grant may make it a holder whose run\n   ends in an exit.\"\n  [config units]\n  (check! (some? (:me config)) \"an identity: the holder a grant would name\"\n          {:me (:me config)})\n  (check! (and (map? (:protection config))\n               (every? classes (vals (:protection config))))\n          \"a protection declaration over the three classes\"\n          {:protection (:protection config)})\n  (check! (map? (:receiver config)) \"a receiving machine\" {})\n  (check-interval! units (:renewal-interval config))\n  (doseq [s seams]\n    (check! (fn? (get config s)) (str \"the seam \" (name s)) {}))\n  (doseq [key (vals inbox/lanes)]\n    (inbox/check-descriptor! (get config key)))\n  (doseq [s optional-seams\n          :when (some? (get config s))]\n    (check! (fn? (get config s)) (str \"the seam \" (name s)) {}))\n  (check! (some? (get config :journal))\n          \"a progress journal: a dao.stream.journal handle\" {})\n  (check! (and (map? (:store config))\n               (fn? (:put-bytes-fn (:store config)))\n               (fn? (:get-bytes-fn (:store config))))\n          \"a content store: a dao.jing byte-store handle\"\n          {})\n  (check! (some? (:medium config)) \"a carrier medium name\" {}))\n\n\n(defn- bare-state\n  \"The step state every constructor fills from `config`.\"\n  [config units]\n  {:phase :validating\n   :config (select-keys config [:yin.k/admitted-modules])\n   :status nil\n   :detail nil\n   :me (:me config)\n   :bytes (:bytes config)\n   :address (:address config)\n   :protection (:protection config)\n   :renewal-interval (:renewal-interval config)\n   :units units\n   :receiver (:receiver config)\n   :seams (-> (select-keys config (into (into seams optional-seams) (vals inbox/lanes)))\n              (assoc :journal (:journal config)\n                     :content-store (:store config)\n                     :medium (:medium config)))\n   :occurrence nil\n   :arbitration nil\n   :kind nil\n   :proposal nil\n   :proposals 0\n   :sent-proposals #{}\n   :lease nil\n   :holder nil\n   :evidence nil\n   :machine nil\n   :renewals 0\n   :renewal-pending? false\n   :release nil\n   The bracket itself opens through `release!` -- for an abort whose\n   journal append crossed the lease bound it opens only with the next\n   step, so the abort's terminal record is the journal's last word of\n   the call that wrote it.  This is cleanup, never recovery: it clears\n   no quarantine, completes nothing without accepted completion\n   evidence, and the occurrence of an ordinary failed run stays\n   regrantable under the ordinary policy.\"\n  [state status detail]\n  (-> state\n      (assoc :phase :releasing\n             :status status\n             :detail detail\n             :machine (some-> (:machine state) ended)\n             :holder (some-> (:holder state)\n                             (as-> h (when (some? (:grant h))\n                                       (:holder (lease/stop h)))))\n             :release nil)))\n\n\n(defn- releasing\n  \"`state` entering :releasing over lease `l`, carrying the failure it\n   will answer once the release has left: any machine is gated ended,\n   the dao.lease holder has stopped, and the release bracket opens --\n   its intent journaled before the send, the identical request retried\n   until authenticated carriage.  This is cleanup, never recovery: it\n   clears no quarantine, completes nothing without accepted completion\n   evidence, and the occurrence of an ordinary failed run stays\n   regrantable under the ordinary policy.\"\n  [state status detail l]\n  (release! (releasing-state state status detail) l))\n\n\n(defn- end-run\n  \"End the run (r3 1.6): all program IO stops, the machine is gated\n   ended, one diagnostic is appended to the composition's diagnostic\n   stream, and the lease is released while it may still be live.  A\n   run end the state already carries -- the cycle's own, or a record's\n   earlier in the same drain -- folds into that one diagnostic under\n   :also-ended, never a second diagnostic.\"\n  [state cause detail]\n  (let [also (some-> (:run-end state) :cause)\n        detail (if (some? also) (assoc detail :also-ended also) detail)\n        diagnostic {:yin.k/diagnostic :yin.k/run-ended\n                    :yin.k/occurrence (:occurrence state)\n                    :dao.lease/lease (:lease state)\n                    :yin.k/end (merge {:cause cause} detail)}]\n    (append-diagnostic! state diagnostic)\n    (releasing (-> state (dissoc :run-end) (update :diagnostics conj diagnostic))\n               :yin.k/ended (merge {:cause cause} detail) (:lease state))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The candidate loop: propose, observe the grant, accept it whole\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- proposal-request\n  [occurrence pid]\n  {:yin.k/request :yin.k/proposal\n   :yin.k/request-id [:yin.k/proposal occurrence pid]\n   :dao.lease/proposal pid\n   :yin.k/occurrence occurrence})\n\n\n(defn- fresh-pid\n  [_state]\n  (str (random-uuid)))\n\n\n(defn- propose\n  \"Send or resend the current proposal, minting a fresh id when the last\n   one was refused (7.7.8: a proposal id is answered at most once), and\n   answer the state after it: while it is unanswered the step answers\n   `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`.  A minted id's intent record is durable in\n   the journal before its first send, so a resent proposal keeps its\n   stable id across a reopen.  A stopped candidate never proposes\n   again, and an unavailable inbox holds the proposal until a scan\n   succeeds: either way the outstanding proposal waits passively, and\n   a late grant to it is still observed from the ledger.\"\n  [state projection]\n  (cond\n    (and (:stopped? state)\n         (or (nil? (:proposal state))\n             (= :dao.lease/rejected (get-in projection [:answered [(:me state) (get-in state [:proposal :id])]]))))\n    (with-status state :yin.k/ended {:cause :stopped})\n\n    (or (:stopped? state) (:inbox-unavailable? state))\n      state)))\n\n\n(defn- drain\n  [state mode]\n  (let [state (if (contains? #{:program :control-buffered} mode) state (collect-inbox state))]\n    (if (or (contains? #{:control :control-buffered} mode) (:stopped? state) (:inbox-unavailable? state)\n            (not (contains? #{:running :safepoint} (:phase state))))\n      state\n      (apply-program-inbox state))))\n\n\n(defn- activate\n  [state]\n  (let [{checked :state projection :projection proof :proof} (binding-check state)]\n    (if-not projection\n      checked\n      (let [checked (assoc checked :evidence proof)\n            result (lower checked (:lease checked) (:holder checked)\n                          proof (clock checked))]\n        (if (= :ok (:status result))\n          (assoc checked :phase :running :machine (:vm result))\n          (releasing checked (:yin.k/status result)\n                     (dissoc result :yin.k/status) (:lease checked)))))))\n\n\n(defn- require-tenure!\n  [state]\n  (when-not (lease/holding? (:holder state) (clock state))\n    (throw (ex-info \"Program IO past lease bound\" {::cause :lease-bound}))))\n\n\n(defn- guarded-handle\n  [state handle]\n  (reify\n    stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\n    (descriptor [_] (stream/descriptor handle))\n\n\n    stream/IDaoStreamWriter\n\n    (append!\n      [_ value]\n      (require-tenure! state)\n      (stream/append! handle value))\n\n\n    stream/IDaoStreamClosable\n\n    (close!\n      [_]\n      (require-tenure! state)\n      (stream/close! handle))))\n\n\n(defn- guard-writes\n  [state machine]\n  (reduce (fn [root [path task]]\n            (assoc-in root (conj path :resources)\n                      (into {} (map (fn [[resource handle]]\n                                      [resource\n                                       (if (satisfies? stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor handle)\n                                         (guarded-handle state handle) handle)]))\n                            (:resources task))))\n          machine (machines machine)))\n\n\n(defn- restore-handles\n  [machine original]\n  (reduce (fn [root [path task]]\n            (reduce (fn [root [resource handle]]\n                      (if (satisfies? stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor handle)\n                        (assoc-in root (into path [:resources resource]) handle)\n                        root)) root (:resources task)))\n          machine (machines original)))\n\n\n(defn- run-cycle\n  \"One program cycle of the run, after tenure: the machine's internal\n   computation, replay below the frontier, the writer's emit, the\n   reader's live step.  The unsatisfied answers of the writer and the\n   reader ride the state as :step-unsatisfied; a run end anywhere\n   rides it as :run-end.\"\n  [state]\n  (let [_ (require-tenure! state)\n        m0 (:machine state)\n        m1 (if (and (= :running (vm/gate-mode m0)) (not (:halted? m0)))\n             (vm/run m0)\n             m0)\n        _ (require-tenure! state)\n        replay (reader/replay m1 {:control-outstanding?\n                                  (control-outstanding? state)})\n        m2 (:machine replay)\n        _ (require-tenure! state)\n        request! (fn [request] (require-tenure! state) (append-request! state request))\n        emit (writer/emit (guard-writes state m2) request!\n                          (fn [diagnostic]\n                            (require-tenure! state)\n                            (append-diagnostic! state diagnostic)))\n        m3 (restore-handles (:machine emit) m2)\n        live (reader/step m3 request!\n                          (fn [h op arg]\n                            (require-tenure! state)\n                            (observe! state h op arg)))\n        m4 (:machine live)]\n    (assoc state\n           :machine m4\n           :run-end (or (:run-end replay) (:run-end emit) (:run-end live))\n           :step-unsatisfied (or (:unsatisfied replay)\n                                 (:unsatisfied emit)\n                                 (:unsatisfied live)))))\n\n\n(defn- at-safepoint?\n  \"True when the machine has nothing left the candidate half owes it:\n   halted with no wait entry and no queued work anywhere in the tree.\"\n  [machine]\n  (and (:halted? machine)\n       (every? (fn [[_ m]]\n                 (and (empty? (:wait-set m)) (empty? (:ready-queue m))))\n               (machines machine))))\n\n\n(defn- exit-settled?\n  \"True when the exit's release bracket is closed: the report stands\n   and the release has been carried, so the closure is the grantor's\n   transition and no tenure of this driver's can change it.\"\n  [state]\n  (and (map? (:exit state))\n       (true? (get-in state [:release :carried]))))\n\n\n(defn- cycle-result\n  [state]\n  (let [run-end (:run-end state)\n        unsatisfied-step (:step-unsatisfied state)\n        base (dissoc state :run-end :step-unsatisfied)]\n    (cond\n      (= :releasing (:phase base)) base\n      run-end (end-run base (:cause run-end) (dissoc run-end :cause))\n      (:inbox-unavailable? base) base\n      unsatisfied-step (with-status base :yin.k/unsatisfied\n                         (dissoc unsatisfied-step :yin.k/status))\n      (at-safepoint? (:machine base))\n      (let [{:keys [state refusal]} (arm-exit base :yin.k/result)]\n        (cond-> (assoc state :phase :safepoint)\n          (nil? refusal)\n          (assoc :status :yin.k/ok\n                 :detail {:yin.k/occurrence (:occurrence state)\n                          :dao.lease/lease (:lease state)})))\n      :else (with-status base :yin.k/ok\n              {:yin.k/occurrence (:occurrence base) :dao.lease/lease (:lease base)}))))\n\n\n(defn- step-active-held\n  \"Share binding validation and tenure between control and program scheduling.\n   Only program scheduling applies retained results or prepares an export.\"\n  [state mode]\n  (let [state (if (or (= :program mode) (nil? (:lease state))) state (collect-inbox state))]\n    (cond\n      (:inbox-refused? state) state\n      (= :releasing (:phase state)) state\n      (nil? (:lease state)) state\n      (and (:stopped? state) (nil? (get-in state [:exit :address])))\n      (end-run state :stopped {:dao.lease/lease (:lease state)})\n      (exit-settled? state)\n      (if (= :program mode) state (step-exiting state))\n      :else\n      (let [{checked :state projection :projection} (binding-check state)]\n        (if-not projection\n          checked\n          (let [drained (drain (dissoc checked :program-suspended?)\n                               (if (= :control mode) :control-buffered :program))]\n            (cond\n              (= :releasing (:phase drained)) (dissoc drained :run-end)\n              (:program-suspended? drained) (dissoc drained :program-suspended?)\n              (:run-end drained) (end-run (dissoc drained :run-end)\n                                          (get-in drained [:run-end :cause])\n                                          (dissoc (:run-end drained) :cause))\n              :else\n              (let [reading (clock drained)\n                    _ (require-tenure! drained)\n                    [renewed holder] (if (= :program mode)\n                                       [drained (:holder drained)]\n                                       (renew drained (:holder drained) reading))\n                    state (assoc renewed :holder holder)\n                    armed? (some? (:exit state))\n                    preparing? (nil? (get-in state [:exit :address]))]\n                (cond\n                  (:inbox-unavailable? state)\n                  (if (and armed? (not preparing?) (not= :program mode))\n                    (let [progressed (drain (step-exit-body state projection) mode)]\n                      (if (and (:inbox-unavailable? progressed) (= :yin.k/ok (:status progressed)))\n                        (with-status progressed (:status state) (:detail state))\n                        progressed))\n                    state)\n                  (and armed?\n                       (or (and (= :control mode) preparing?)\n                           (and (= :program mode) (not preparing?)))) state\n                  armed? (drain (step-exit-body state projection) mode)\n                  (or (= :control mode) (= :activating (:phase state)))\n                  (with-status state :yin.k/ok\n                    {:yin.k/occurrence (:occurrence state) :dao.lease/lease (:lease state)})\n                  :else (cycle-result (drain (run-cycle state) mode)))))))))))\n\n\n(defn- step-active\n  \"The held step under the catch: every seam the cycle runs through\n   answers an outcome and never throws (the seam contract of\n   `initial`), so a throw here is the cycle's own -- a tenure guard or\n   a program effect -- and it ends the run.  An uncertain journal\n   append is none of these: it unwinds untouched, and `step` stalls\n   everything on it.\"\n  [state mode]\n  (try (step-active-held state mode)\n       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) failure\n         (if (or (::journal-uncertain (ex-data failure)) (::inbox/refusal (ex-data failure)))\n           (throw failure)\n           (if (= :lease-bound (::cause (ex-data failure)))\n             (end-run state :lease-bound {:dao.lease/lease (:lease state)})\n             (end-run state :program-failure\n                      {:failure {:message (ex-message failure)\n                                 :data (ex-data failure)}}))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The release step\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- step-releasing\n  \"A pending release is retried with the identical request until\n   authenticated carriage is acknowledged, whose acknowledgment is\n   journaled.  A release a restarted holder owes (:re-propose?)\n   re-enters as a candidate once it has left, instead of answering the\n   failure the dead run died with.\"\n  [state]\n  (let [state (drain-control state)\n        release (:release state)]\n    (if (:inbox-refused? state)\n      state\n      (if (true? (:carried release))\n        (if (and (:re-propose? release) (not (:stopped? state)))\n          (-> state\n              (dissoc :release :holder :evidence)\n              (assoc :phase :proposing :proposal nil)\n              (with-status :yin.k/awaiting-grant\n                {:yin.k/occurrence (:occurrence state)}))\n          (-> state\n              (assoc :phase :failed)\n              (update :detail assoc :dao.lease/released true)))\n        (release! state (:lease release))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Validate: the D7 pipeline with zero side effects\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- step-validating\n  [state]\n  (let [b (checkpoint/inspect (:address state) (:bytes state))\n        r (if (:yin.k/status b) b (lowered state {:exclusive true}))]\n    (cond\n      ;; a valid blocked or parked root answers awaiting-grant at\n      ;; the custody gate, before any attachment\n      (= :yin.k/awaiting-grant (:yin.k/status r))\n      (assoc state :phase :proposing\n             :occurrence (:yin.k/occurrence b)\n             :arbitration (get-in b\n                                  [:yin.k/arbitration\n                                   :dao.stream/identity])\n             :kind (:yin.k/kind b))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
