{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '315,350p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md; sed -n '373,417p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md; sed -n '569,586p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md; sed -n '615,632p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md; sed -n '1,160p' docs/agents/build-n-test.md; sed -n '2310,2360p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"\n**§3.2**: S1, C2, C3, R1, R2, P1 as plan v2 §3.1's validator table (R3 is folded into S1: a `run` with a `deliver` or `pending` key is `:frame-shape`). \"`window-defect` is parameterised by the wanted set: `saved(pc, rd)` for an act and a return frame, `live(pc)` for a run; there is no `:window-self` analogue.\"\n\n**§3.3**: step 7 judges \"every wait's state in context `:wait`, the root's `:run` in context `:running`, every `:ready`'s wait in context `:wait`, every parked state in `:parked`, every reified value in `:captured`\".\n\n**§4.3** (stack layouts), replace \"**Checked** (§5.6 item 5) … dead layout syntax).\" with:\n\n> **Defined at every pc of every body.** `stack-layout(p)` is the ancestors' pending pushes for a parse node's pc; for a conditional's `:jump` pc `j` it is `stack-layout(b) ++ [rd(if)]` (`b` the branch pc, `rd(if)` the register both arms define: the consequent's value is on the stack while control jumps); for a body's terminator (`:return r`, `:halt r`) it is `[]`. **Checked** (§5.6 item 5), ordered: the current region is `stack-layout(p) ++ [W[u] for u in uses(p)]` in emitter order (operator then operands; `:stream-put` target then value), and as sets `stack-layout(p) ∪ uses(p) = L(p)` at every reachable non-tail pc, `uses(p) = L(p)` at every reachable tail-call pc. **Dead prefix.** A tail `:call` consumes its operator and operands and keeps the rest of its region without pushing a frame (`stack.cljc:731`, `:747`), so a region entered through a tail call may hold, below the layout, values the ambient layout syntax pushed and nothing reads again (every read is top-relative; a return truncates to a base that includes them). The lift takes the top `|stack-layout(p)| + |uses(p)|` values of the current region and the top `|stack-layout(pc_i − 1)|` of each frame's slice and drops the prefix; the lower produces none and recomputes bases cumulatively; the prefix is unobservable by law 1 and absent from the canonical form by laws 2 and 4. A `:push` pc of H is a stutter: its state equals its successor's but for pc, and the lift relabels it to `h->a(hpc+1)` without running anything.\n\n**§4.5**, last clause → \"never invents a state: a kernel pc outside every body is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2, as amended; the name is historical); every other kernel pc lifts, as a delivery state when the kernel is parked and as a `run` state when it is between instructions (6e); a `:push` pc of H is relabelled to its successor. Until 6e lands, a de Bruijn kernel with an active continuation is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` as a documented limit of the backends, not of the carrier.\"\n\n**§5.4 law 4**: \"one program at one **aligned instruction boundary** (its parking boundaries included; the stack lane's `:push` stutters relabelled) lifts to row sets whose root ids are equal …\". Laws 1, 2, 6: \"at every boundary of the stepping driver (§5.7)\".\n\n**§5.6**, items 3, 5, 10:\n\n> 3. … at every reachable pc `p`, `virt(p, s)` is single-valued on the physical image of `L(p)`; the `live-out*(p)` clause stays for parked entries.\n> 5. … the ordered region check and the set equations of §4.3 at every reachable pc; the parked-boundary form `(set (stack-layout p)) = saved(p+1, rd)` stays; duplicate operands are two positions holding one value and the lift asserts they agree.\n> 10. Stepping: for every case the reference lane's boundary count to the first park and to the halt is pinned per host; the three lanes agree on it after the stack lane's `:push` relabelling (`correspondence`).\n\n**New §5.7 \"The stepping driver\"** = plan v2 §3.6 (order, isolated worlds, coverage classes, \"every pc\" definition) in design voice.\n\n**§7.1 steps 1-2**, replace:\n\n> 1. **Holds.** Refuse `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch` on an observation in flight (an `:observe` wait, a held entry, an unminted cursor, a pending close, a link request without its cursor): these are the holder's own seams mid-step, resolved by the driver's next cycle without executing guest code; they are not task states at rest. There is no quiescence refusal: a non-empty ready queue is carried (§2.3 `:ready`).\n> 2. **Kind.** `:running` when `:control` is set; else `:parked` when the value is a parked continuation the parked table holds; else `:blocked` when the wait set or the ready queue is non-empty; else `:halted` with empty wait set and queue; anything else is `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` (unreachable by construction).\n\nand in step 4: \"a `:running` task's state node is the control's `run` state with the window trimmed to `L(pc)` (`select-keys`), contributing the context `(A, pc, E)` to discharge (`live-pcs` from `pc`); each ready entry contributes its wait's state as a wait does, its value through the value walk and its cursor update through the census\". Delete \"`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` when `:control` is set\" from the `canonical-task` paragraph: \"a kernel with an active continuation is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` until 6e, then a `run` state through `lift-running`\".\n\n**§7.2**, restore: \"`:running`: `:control {:segment local :pc pc} :window W :env E :k (when (seq K) K) :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil :wait-set entries`; every kind: `:ready-queue` from the ready rows in order, each entry its wait plus `:value`, `:status` and `:resource-updates` re-keyed to the receiver's cells; nothing runs; `vm/run` or `run-fuel` continues from `pc`, or sweeps and resumes the first ready entry.\"\n\n**New §7.3 \"Custody and lifecycle of a running root\"** = plan v2 §3.3 in design voice, including the four outcomes, the sentence \"progress since the last durable checkpoint is lost on expiry; fuel makes the computation interruptible, exporting before the bound is what preserves it\", the gate guard, and \"a completed `:put` wake is a completed operation: its op-id is well-formed but not a retained write\".\n\n**New §7.4 \"Fuel\"** = plan v2 §3.4 including the honest guarantee verbatim.\n\n**§8.3**, item 12: \"`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` from a kernel pc outside every body; a kernel stepped to a non-parking pc lifts as a `run` state (6e; until then the backend refusal is pinned as a limit); a `:push` pc relabels\". Add §8.6 (6d-a), §8.7 (6d-b), §8.8 (6e) = plan v2 §3.9.\n\n#### 3.7.3 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`\n\n**Blocker 1 (`:17`)**: append \"**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc):** the parking transitions are where the program stops by itself; the set of liftable configurations is every instruction boundary of the register-shaped machine together with its scheduler state (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1), and the safepoint table enumerates the delivery states within it.\"\n\n**§7.2.1 (`:264-266`)**: prefix \"(versions 0-2; version 3 admits `run` states at every pc and completed wakes, continuation design §1.3)\".\n\n**§7.4.1 title and rule.** Title → \"7.4.1 Every instruction boundary is representable; the safepoint table is the machine's parking transitions\". Replace \"UCF resolves this by rule rather than by metadata: **a continuation may be lifted only at a safepoint**, and a safepoint is a transition of the reference machine at which the machine parks.\" with:\n\n> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17).** The register-shaped machine has no unserializable interior: its task state between any two instructions, `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` with its ordered waits and completed wakes, is canonical (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1; continuation design §1.3). **A continuation is representable at every instruction boundary**; the lift refuses a value with no wire form, a dependency the receiver cannot satisfy, or an observation in flight, never a pc. A *safepoint* is a boundary at which the program stops by itself: a park with a pending wait, an explicit park with none, or a halt with a result. The paragraph above describes the stack-shaped machine, which left at phase 8.\n\nKeyed-form block: add the `run` state. **Quiescence** paragraph (`:762-765`): replace with \"There is no quiescence requirement: a completed wake in the ready queue travels as a `:ready` row with its value, status and cursor update, and the receiver resumes it before any newly woken wait, in carried order.\"\n\n**§7.4.2** (`:800-825`): the conformance sentence (`:802`) → \"by a conformance test that runs the segment to **every boundary of the stepping driver** and compares the saved window's key set with `live − {rd}` at parks, the run window's key set with `live` between instructions, and the E-reads\"; rules → \"`lift(engine-state, layout) → canonical frame` and `lower(canonical frame, layout) → engine-state` are total on **every pc of A** the engine reaches; a pc that is no pc of A relabels (a `:push` of the stack image) or refuses (outside every body)\". Replace the `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` bullet with:\n\n> - A request to lift at a pc outside every body returns `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` naming the pc; the name is historical, the meaning is \"no state of A exists here\". A stack image's `:push` pc is relabelled to its successor's A pc without running anything. An engine may not invent states a record does not have, and two engines that disagree about the state set of one address are not both conforming. An engine that cannot hold a value of the canonical frame refuses before attachment with its own kind (the register backend's `:continuation-registers`); that is a limit of the engine, not of the address.\n\nLast paragraph (`:823-825`): \"a debugger's breakpoint set is any subset of the pcs of A; the safepoint set is where the program will stop by itself\".\n\n**§7.7.4** step 3 (`:1901-1903`) → \"Neutralize control: a parked or blocked machine has no control; a machine between instructions (root kind `:running`) keeps its `:control`, `:window`, `:env` and `:k` as the state the lift reads, and the kernel refuses to step a machine whose gate is `:exporting` or `:ended` (`yin.vm.semantic/vm-hot`, one check at entry, outside the instruction loop), so the exporting machine is pure data in both cases; direct `:resume` …\". `:1912-1913` \"pure data with an empty wait-set and a retained export record — no engine change, no callback, no timer\" → \"pure data with its wait set, ready queue and parked records moved into the retained export record — one engine change at the kernel's entry (the gate guard), no change to the instruction loop, no callback, no timer\". `:1941-1942` \"A task is not at a liftable safepoint while it holds any of these\" → \"A task is not export-ready while it holds any of these; they are the driver's own seams and resolve in its next cycle without guest execution.\" In the \"(M-next D8/D9, as built.)\" paragraph, after \"under `:running` internal computation proceeds\" add \"for the holder's fuel budget per program tick (`driver/run-cycle`, `:fuel`), so a long computation is preempted at an instruction boundary and may be exported there; a holder whose tenure ends keeps the newest completed pure state for inspection and offers nothing; the occurrence is regrantable from its last durable checkpoint and progress since it is lost (continuation design §7.3)\".\n\n**§7.7.6** (`:2161`) \"runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\" → \"runs to its next park, halt, or fuel boundary\".\n\n**§7.8 Lift** step 1 (`:2621-2623`) → \"The machine is parked, blocked, halted or between instructions (`:running`), with a local record `r`, and holds no observation in flight, else `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch`.\" Step 3 (`:2627-2628`) → \"`lift(export-record)`: confirm every pc is a pc of A (§7.4.1), else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; …\". Step 9 (`:2673`) → \"At the next park, halt, or fuel boundary, produce the successor occurrence …\".\n\n**§7.9** (`:2690`): `| :yin.k/not-at-safepoint | lift | :yin.k/segment, :yin.k/pc: the pc is outside every body of A |`; delete the `:yin.k/not-quiescent` row.\n\n**§7.10** (`:2839`): \"Resume happens only at an instruction boundary the canonical vector defines (a parking transition, or the boundary a holder's fuel budget stopped at); the successor chain …\". **`:2842` No layer collapsing** → \"The engine's instruction loop is untouched: lifting is a pure function over the machine's value at any boundary, the one engine change is a gate check at the kernel's entry, and the exporting state is that value with its waits, queue and parked records moved into a retained record — driver discipline, not engine machinery.\"\n\n**§7.11** (`:2931-2942`): rename \"Continuation conformance harness\"; \"to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table — effect-producing calls included —\" → \"to **every boundary of the stepping driver** (continuation design §5.7), the complete §7.4.1 table's parking transitions included,\"; the cross-engine sentence \"one program at one aligned instruction boundary\". **§7.11.1** (`:3100-3130`): Invariant → \"At every instruction boundary, lifting and lowering preserve the next observable step (and, at a parking transition, its wait; with a non-empty queue, its order); a pc outside every body refuses.\" Setup: \"a nonsafepoint pc\" → \"a `:push` pc of the stack image (relabels) and a body-start pc (lifts)\"; expected: \"An undeclared pc gives `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; queued work gives `:yin.k/not-quiescent`\" → \"A pc outside every body gives `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; every pc of A lifts as a `run` state when the machine is between instructions; queued work travels as `:ready` rows and resumes in order\".\n\n#### 3.7.4 `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`\n\n**§1 stance item 1** (`:45-49`): append \"Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc): a *safepoint* is a parking `(segment-address, pc)`; the carrier expresses every `(segment-address, pc)` of A as a `:run` state when the machine is between instructions, and the scheduler's completed wakes as `:ready` rows.\" **§5.2**: `:run` and `:ready` rows; `:wait`'s note. **§5.5** (`:455-460`): the kind-rules paragraph = plan v2 §3.1 table; keep \"Frames restore in carried order and are never collapsed to one; a task may hold several ordered waits **and several ordered completed wakes**\". Custody table (`:466-473`): rows `root | running / blocked / parked | exclusive | the four keys plus origin on a successor` and `root | running / blocked / parked | fork | nil`; add \"a completed `:put` wake is a completed operation: its op-id is well-formed, not retained\". **§8 step 7** (`:797-801`): contexts \"(`:wait`, `:captured`, `:parked`, `:running`)\" and \"the root's `:run` and each `:ready`'s wait\". **§9 Lift** (`:880`): \"the merged order is holds, kind, free environment …\" (quiescence deleted); the canonical round-trip law (`:913-917`) unchanged (\"no execution between lower and lift\" is the law the harness now follows literally); **Cross-engine** (`:929`): \"Running the corpus to each aligned instruction boundary\". **§11** (`:1027`): \"Code identity (A), the parking transitions,\".\n\n#### 3.7.5 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md` §7 (`:560-579`)\n\nReplace \"Only blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks at the existing engine export boundary are eligible. Running CESK/PC snapshots are not a fourth kind.\" with:\n\n> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc).** For the stack-shaped machine of versions 0-2, only blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks were eligible and running snapshots were not a fourth kind. For the register-shaped machine and body version 3 this is reversed by the owner's invariant: a task between instructions is the fourth kind, `:running`, carried with its ordered waits and completed wakes (continuation design §1.3, §2.6), and is eligible for export and custody.\n\n#### 3.7.6 `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` §14.1\n\nTable 14.1.1 (`:3777-3788`): add `| Between instructions (:running) | Active control; ordered waits and completed wakes travel; the window is exactly L(pc); continue at pc. |`. `:3798` \"Static `:yin.safepoint/kinds` checks pc eligibility only\" → \"…checks a parking pc's kinds; a `run` pc is any pc of a body\". `:3827` → \"Validate the instruction boundary (§7.4.1 as amended); enter exporting before …\". `:3882` \"continue to the next safepoint\" → \"continue to the next park, halt or fuel boundary\". `:3900-3901` → \"A pc outside every body yields `not-at-safepoint`; queued work travels as `:ready` rows.\" `:4145` as UCF §7.8 step 9.\n\n#### 3.7.7 `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`\n#### 6d-b: holder, custody, expiry, recovery\n\n**Change.**\n1. `driver.cljc` `run-cycle` (`:2111-2144`): `:fuel` config key (checked in `check-common!` `:177-207`, positive integer or nil); `(if fuel (sr/run-fuel m0 fuel) (vm/run m0))` under the existing gate and halted guards; **each stage's machine assoc'd into the state before the next tenure check**; `cycle-result` adds `:yin.k/at-boundary true`; `step-active`'s catch ends the run on the state it was handed and records `:yin.k/retained-boundary`; `end-run` keeps the retained machine on the ended state (gated `:ended`, never offered).\n2. `export.cljc`: `kind-of` (`:274-282`) `:running` first; `enter` (`:121-149`) drops `first-refusal` and moves the ready queue into the record (`:ready []` on the machine; `:yin.k/recovery-ready` on fenced restore); `recovery-metadata`/`valid-state?` count ready entries; `abort` unchanged (its conditions are right).\n3. `semantic.cljc` `vm-hot` entry gate guard (§3.3 outcome 4); `run-fuel` inherits it.\n4. `ledger.cljc:277`, `authority/grant.cljc:74-75`: admit `:running`.\n5. Docstrings per §3.8 (driver, compose, export header).\n6. Tests: `driver_test`: a fuelled run preempts a long pure loop at a boundary, the phase stays `:running`, the next tick continues; `hand-off` mints, journals, freezes and offers a `:running` pack; a candidate lowers it (awaiting-grant → grant → `activate`) and reaches the reference result; the five clock-controlled cuts of §3.3 with their assertions; `rehydrate-fenced` byte equality for a running root with a wait and with a ready entry, and the rehydrated machine refuses `vm/step` (\"Gate is closed\"); two competing candidates; stale-source stepping refused; uncertain-offer abort refused; expired-grant admission refused; a halted exclusive root still restores without a grant (F6 regression). `ledger_test`/`grant_test`: a `:running` baseline is offerable; `:halted` is not. `compose_rows_test`: an exclusive world handed off at a boundary completes on the successor; one with a running install child. `stage_e_scenarios`: `[:until \"h1\" :running n] [:hand-off \"h1\"]` with fuel so the hand-off lands mid-body; recovery cut before and after the offer; a tenure-expiry cut between slices asserting the last checkpoint is what recovers.\n7. Docs: §3.7.1 §7.3; §3.7.3 UCF §7.7.4, §7.7.6, §7.8, §7.10, §7.11, §7.11.1, `:2842`; §3.7.6.\n\n**Depends on.** 6d-a. **Days.** 2.5 + 1.5 (3 rounds) = 4.\n\n#### 6e: the cross-engine half\n\n**Change.** `layout.cljc`: `lift-running`/`lower-running` over the kernels' live fields (register `{:pc :registers :frames :continuation :store-of}`, stack `{:pc :stack :frames :continuation :store-of}`); `virt` over a given virtual set (`L(p)` for a run); `register-window` parameterised; nil fill outside `L(p)`; `h->a` relabels `:push`; `slice-window`/`lift-stack` dead-prefix rule (`≥`, prefix dropped, diagnostic); `lower-stack`/`stack-k` unchanged in shape; `safepoint!` run mode (any pc inside a body); `canonical-task` lifts an active kernel as `:running` (with waits and ready as the semantic lift does; backends still refuse installs); `kernel-task` lowers a `:running` machine into a running kernel. No kernel edit. `layout_test`, the harness: laws 4 and 5 at every aligned boundary on the 61 lane cases.\n\n**Acceptance.** Law 4: one root id on three lanes at aligned boundary `i` (stack index after `:push` relabelling), taken and untaken branches both pinned, ordered operand multiplicity pinned; law 5: six ordered pairs at every boundary, each continuing to the reference result, with the **full-payload refusal oracle** of §3.6 (exact refusals and exact successful transfers per case and direction); the `:push` relabelling pinned on one row; **item 6, tail residuals**: either a lane-fragment case with a non-empty ambient layout at a tail call exercises the dead-prefix rule (lift drops, lower omits, law 1 and law 4 hold, the diagnostic reports the count) or the static check `layout(p) = []` at every tail-call pc of the fragment proves none exists and the rule is pinned on a hand-built vector; `layout-refusals` rewritten: a kernel stepped to a non-parking pc lifts on both lanes with zero serves refused; a hand-built entry outside every body refuses `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; the fragment is not widened (rows 7-12 and 17 excluded; row 4 not lowered).\n### 3.11 Risks\n\n1. **A liveness bug surfaces** at a pc no park looked at: law 1 fails there. A finding, not a slice risk; the main reason to run the every-boundary laws.\n2. **The ready carrier meets a wake reason I did not enumerate** (a link wake entering the queue): pinned as `:ready-reason` refusal in 6d-a and reported; widening is a small follow-up, not a redesign.\n3. **Re-mint churn**: two re-mints in a week (module-pins, 6d-a). Mitigation: order (§3.9); 6d's own acceptance pins boundary counts and refusal kinds, never root ids.\n4. **Dead-prefix rule untested on a real case** if the fragment has none: 6e item 6 pins it on a hand-built vector; the rule is then a documented check awaiting a natural case.\n5. **Slow-tier time on Dart**: slow tier only; measured once.\n6. **Gate guard cost**: one map read per `run-fuel`/`vm-hot` call; a reviewer confirms it is outside the loop.\n7. **Fuel too small or too large**: a composition knob; default nil.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Verification honesty\n\n- Every `file:line` above was read in `7c4dff32` today. The review's citations held in every case I checked; where it cited a function by name I read the body.\n- **Run**: one throwaway JVM test namespace reproducing F1, F2 and F5 (transcript excerpts in §1); deleted; `git status` shows only `collab/`. **Not run**: the harness, the slow tier, any Node or Dart lane, any driver or Stage E scenario, any performance probe. Boundary counts and point counts are to be measured once and pinned by 6d-a.\n- **Claims that are checks** (not theorems): `L(p)` sufficient at every boundary; injectivity over `L(p)` at every pc; the ordered stack equation with the jump/terminator layouts at every pc; the dead-prefix rule; the refusal-oracle equality; cross-host boundary-count equality; the per-tree fuel bound.\n- **Not claimed**: that the de Bruijn kernels reconstruct a `run` state today (6e); that the holder can preempt today (`vm/run` at `driver.cljc:2120`); that fuel bounds wall-clock time; that fuel repairs expiry loss (only exporting before the bound does); that a tail call with a non-empty ambient layout exists in the fragment (6e decides); that link wakes never enter the queue (6d-a verifies by reading `poll-links`); that module-pins commit 2's final hunks are exactly those of `origin/srvm-module-pins` at `62ed14fa` (they are what is merging now).\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n- The envelope names no target, no effect kind, and no intent. The\n  consumer takes the target identity and the effect kind from the\n  boundary the envelope arrived at, and computes intent itself.\n- The envelope carries no credential. The holder is authenticated\n  by the attribution the composition supplies (`dao.lease.md`,\n  *Composition duties*). An envelope whose attributed author is not\n  the holder that the binding of `L` names is a fact about that\n  author: it is defective.\n\n**Defective envelopes.** A defective envelope commits no effect and\ncreates no dedup record. It yields no admission outcome: an outcome\nwould be attributed to the holder its fields claim, and those fields\nare exactly what cannot be trusted. The consumer instead appends one\nstructured diagnostic to the diagnostic stream the composition\nsupplies:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/diagnostic :yin.k/defective-envelope\n :yin.k/defect     :malformed       ; closed, below\n :yin.k/target     i                ; the boundary's stream identity\n :yin.k/author     a                ; resolved attribution, if any\n :yin.k/claimed    {...}}           ; readable envelope fields\n```\n\n- The closed defect set is `:malformed` for invalid envelope\n  structure, unsupported envelope dispatch or version, invalid\n  numeric fields, or intent that cannot be canonically encoded;\n  `:unbound-lease` when no valid binding establishes the claimed\n  incarnation; `:wrong-author` when attribution is absent, invalid,\n  or does not identify the bound holder; and `:foreign-op-id` when\n  authoritative scope validation fails.\n- (M-next C, slice C7.) An op id whose `:yin.k/seq` is 2^52-1, a\n  value no writer can assign, is `:malformed`.\n- Enrollment belongs to the target boundary, while a writer's\n  authorization comes from attribution and binding. There is no\n  \"unenrolled writer\" defect: an unenrolled boundary cannot run\n  this admission protocol or claim its guarantee, so it produces\n  neither outcomes nor these diagnostics.\n- Readers dispatch on the `:yin.k/diagnostic` key, never on the\n  presence of an id. The diagnostic adds nothing to a lease fact or\n  to a DaoStream outcome map.\n- The composition supplies the diagnostic stream explicitly.\n  Failure or backpressure while publishing a diagnostic never\n  permits the rejected effect or creates an admission outcome;\n  diagnostic publication failure remains an explicit driver\n  outcome.\n- `:yin.k/claimed` holds whichever of `:yin.k/incarnation`,\n  `:yin.k/epoch` and `:yin.k/op-id` could be read, as claims. They\n  are nested so that no reader correlating on a top-level op id can\n  take the diagnostic for an answer. The payload is never echoed.\n- The map carries neither `:yin.k/admission` nor `:yin.k/status`. A\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '85,310p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc; sed -n '177,207p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc; sed -n '1462,1508p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc; sed -n '2080,2290p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '85,310p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc; sed -n '177,207p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc; sed -n '1462,1508p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc; sed -n '2080,2290p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"   data the refusal carries, or nil.\"\n  [m]\n  (let [waits (:wait-set m)\n        cells (filter map? (vals (:resources m)))]\n    (cond\n      (some #(= :observe (:reason %)) waits)\n      {:yin.k/hold :observe}\n\n      (or (some held-entry? waits) (some held-entry? cells))\n      {:yin.k/hold :held}\n\n      (some :yin.k/unminted cells)\n      {:yin.k/hold :unminted-cursor}\n\n      (seq (:yin.k/closes m))\n      {:yin.k/hold :pending-close}\n\n      (some #(and (= :link-request (:reason %)) (not (contains? % :cursor)))\n            waits)\n      {:yin.k/hold :link-cursor-not-installed})))\n\n\n(defn- first-refusal\n  [vm]\n  (some (fn [[path m]]\n          (when (seq (:ready-queue m))\n            {:yin.k/status :yin.k/not-quiescent\n             :yin.k/path path\n             :yin.k/ready (count (:ready-queue m))}))\n        (machines [] vm)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Entering exporting\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn enter\n  \"Enter exporting from `machine`.  Answers\n   `{:status :ok :machine m :record r}`: `m` is `machine` with the gate\n   `:exporting`, an empty wait set and no parked record, and `r` the\n   export record, holding the wait set in order, every parked record\n   (the row carrier carries them all, continuation §2.5), and the gate\n   to return to.  Otherwise answers the data outcome of the first\n   refusal, leaving `machine` as it was: `:yin.k/not-quiescent` for\n   queued work in the root or a child, a `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind\n   `:reason-mismatch` (naming the hold and the task path), or\n   `:yin.k/refused` for a machine that is already exporting or ended.\"\n  [machine]\n  (cond\n    (contains? #{:exporting :ended} (vm/gate-mode machine))\n    (refused :not-running)\n\n    :else\n    (or (first-refusal machine)\n        (some (fn [[path m]]\n                (when-some [h (refusal-at m)]\n                  (non-portable (assoc h :yin.k/path path))))\n              (machines [] machine))\n        {:status :ok\n         :machine (cond-> (assoc machine :yin.k/gate :exporting :wait-set [])\n                    (contains? machine :parked) (assoc :parked {}))\n         :record {:wait-set (vec (:wait-set machine))\n                  :parked (:parked machine)\n                  :gate (vm/gate-mode machine)\n                  :served {}}})))\n\n\n(defn- reinstated\n  \"`machine`, an exporting machine, carrying `record`'s waits and\n   parked records again: the value the lift reads.\"\n  [machine record]\n  (-> (reduce-kv (fn [restored module-name child-record]\n                   (update-in restored [:installs module-name :vm]\n                              reinstated child-record))\n                 machine (or (:children record) {}))\n      (assoc :wait-set (:wait-set record))\n      (update :parked merge (:parked record))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Prepare, then encode\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- check-header!\n  \"A header is nil (the fork lift) or a map carrying the arbitration,\n   the counter and the enrolled set; anything else is a defect of the\n   caller, not a refusal.  The lift judges the rest.\"\n  [header]\n  (when (some? header)\n    (when-not (and (map? header)\n                   (contains? header :yin.k/arbitration)\n                   (contains? header :yin.k/next-op-seq)\n                   (set? (:yin.k/enrolled header)))\n      (throw (ex-info \"Prepare of a malformed header\"\n                      {:yin.k/hint :malformed-header})))))\n\n\n(defn- lift\n  \"rows/lift-task of the record over `machine`, under the record's header\n   and source index, every stream asked of `keyed`: `{:status :ok :wire\n   w}` or the lift's refusal as data. A throw without the refusal\n   vocabulary is a defect and propagates.\"\n  [machine record keyed]\n  (try\n    {:status :ok\n     :wire (rows/lift-task (reinstated machine record) nil\n                           {:header (:header record)\n                            :source-index (:source-index record)\n                            :serve-keyed keyed})}\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n      (let [d (ex-data e)]\n        (if (and (map? d) (contains? d :yin.k/status)) d (throw e))))))\n\n\n(defn- seeded-by-handle\n  \"handle -> descriptor for every key the retained `table` already\n   answers, resolved through `machine`'s own resource bindings at each\n   key's task path: the alias reuse an interrupted prepare had already\n   proved, reconstructed before any new key is served, so a retry never\n   serves a handle the record already answers under another alias.\n   Handles are never persisted; this mapping lives within the one\n   call.\"\n  [machine table]\n  (let [at (into {} (machines [] machine))]\n    (into {}\n          (keep (fn [[[path id] descriptor]]\n                  (when-some [h (get-in (get at path) [:resources id])]\n                    [h descriptor])))\n          table)))\n\n\n(defn prepare\n  \"Prepare `record`: call `serve!` once per stream of the exporting\n   `machine`, install children included, and answer\n   `{:status :ok :record r}` with every answer retained in `r`, or the\n   data outcome of the lift's refusal with `:record` added, carrying\n   what was served before the refusal so a retry never serves a stream\n   twice -- not even under a resource alias the refused attempt never\n   reached, whose handle the retained table and the machine's bindings\n   still identify.  The row lift serves while it walks, so a refusal it\n   decides after the walk (`:op-seq-exhausted`, `:unprotected-pending`,\n   an op id on an unenrolled stream, the custody grammar) still finds\n   the streams it reached served and retained (phase-7a D4).\n\n   The served table is keyed by `[task-path resource-id]` and holds the\n   descriptor `serve!` answered. This table is plain data, but the live\n   waits can contain authentic values: `freeze` is the persistence seam.\n   A handle is asked at most once, across the calls of one retained\n   record, even when two resources hold it.  `header` is nil for the\n   fork lift, or the exclusive custody header (`rows/lift-task`'s\n   `:header`), kept in the record as `:header`; `source-index` is the\n   composition's `{A #{tree-address}}` (continuation §6.1), kept as\n   `:source-index`.  Prepare mints nothing: the occurrence was minted by\n   the driver before it was called.\"\n  [machine record serve! header source-index]\n  (check-header! header)\n  (let [table (atom (:served record))\n        by-handle (atom (seeded-by-handle machine (:served record)))\n        once (fn [k h]\n               (let [t @table]\n                 (cond\n                   (contains? t k)\n                   (let [served (get t k)]\n                     ;; an alias of an already-served key asks again for\n                     ;; nothing: the handle is known served from here on\n                     (swap! by-handle assoc h served)\n                     served)\n                   (contains? @by-handle h)\n                   (let [served (get @by-handle h)]\n                     ;; a handle already served under another resource id\n                     ;; is the one stream: no second call, and the table\n                     ;; must hold this key too or the record is unprepared\n                     ;; for a resource it reached\n                     (swap! table assoc k served)\n                     served)\n                   :else\n                   (let [served (serve! h)]\n                     ;; a refusal is not an answer: a retry asks again\n                     (when (some? served)\n                       (swap! table assoc k served)\n                       (swap! by-handle assoc h served))\n                     served))))\n        record (assoc record :header header :source-index source-index)\n        r (lift machine record once)]\n    (if (= :ok (:status r))\n      {:status :ok :record (assoc record :served @table)}\n      (assoc r :record (assoc record :served @table)))))\n\n\n(defn- kind-of\n  \"The lifted task's kind, by the lift's own rule.\"\n  [m]\n  (let [value (:value m)]\n    (cond\n      (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value))\n           (contains? (:parked m) (:id value))) :parked\n      (seq (:wait-set m)) :blocked\n      :else :halted)))\n\n\n(defn encode\n  \"The lift of the prepared `record` over `machine`, from a `serve-keyed`\n   that only reads the record, under the header and source index the\n   record retains: `{:status :ok :bytes b :address a :root B :kind k}`,\n   `b` the pack's canonical bytes, `a` the pack address (BLAKE3 of `b`,\n   the storage key), `B` the root id inside it; or the lift's refusal as\n   data.  It allocates and publishes nothing, and one record gives equal\n   bytes every time.  A stream the record has no answer for is a defect\n   of the caller (an unprepared record), not a refusal.\"\n  [machine record]\n  (let [served (:served record)\n        r (lift machine record\n                (fn [k _h]\n                  (if (contains? served k)\n                    (get served k)\n                    (throw (ex-info \"Encode of an unprepared record\"\n                                    {:yin.k/hint :unprepared-stream})))))]\n    (if (= :ok (:status r))\n      (let [wire (:wire r)]\n        {:status :ok\n         :bytes (cbor/encode wire)\n         :address (rows/pack-address wire)\n         :root (:yin.k/root wire)\n         :kind (kind-of (reinstated machine record))})\n      r)))\n\n(defn- check-common!\n  \"The checks every driver constructor runs: the identity, the\n   protection declaration, the receiving machine, the renewal interval,\n   the fn seams, the progress journal, the content store and the carrier\n   medium -- the D14 seams beside the D13 ones.  A candidate that never\n   exits still carries them: a grant may make it a holder whose run\n   ends in an exit.\"\n  [config units]\n  (check! (some? (:me config)) \"an identity: the holder a grant would name\"\n          {:me (:me config)})\n  (check! (and (map? (:protection config))\n               (every? classes (vals (:protection config))))\n          \"a protection declaration over the three classes\"\n          {:protection (:protection config)})\n  (check! (map? (:receiver config)) \"a receiving machine\" {})\n  (check-interval! units (:renewal-interval config))\n  (doseq [s seams]\n    (check! (fn? (get config s)) (str \"the seam \" (name s)) {}))\n  (doseq [key (vals inbox/lanes)]\n    (inbox/check-descriptor! (get config key)))\n  (doseq [s optional-seams\n          :when (some? (get config s))]\n    (check! (fn? (get config s)) (str \"the seam \" (name s)) {}))\n  (check! (some? (get config :journal))\n          \"a progress journal: a dao.stream.journal handle\" {})\n  (check! (and (map? (:store config))\n               (fn? (:put-bytes-fn (:store config)))\n               (fn? (:get-bytes-fn (:store config))))\n          \"a content store: a dao.jing byte-store handle\"\n          {})\n  (check! (some? (:medium config)) \"a carrier medium name\" {}))\n(defn- end-run\n  \"End the run (r3 1.6): all program IO stops, the machine is gated\n   ended, one diagnostic is appended to the composition's diagnostic\n   stream, and the lease is released while it may still be live.  A\n   run end the state already carries -- the cycle's own, or a record's\n   earlier in the same drain -- folds into that one diagnostic under\n   :also-ended, never a second diagnostic.\"\n  [state cause detail]\n  (let [also (some-> (:run-end state) :cause)\n        detail (if (some? also) (assoc detail :also-ended also) detail)\n        diagnostic {:yin.k/diagnostic :yin.k/run-ended\n                    :yin.k/occurrence (:occurrence state)\n                    :dao.lease/lease (:lease state)\n                    :yin.k/end (merge {:cause cause} detail)}]\n    (append-diagnostic! state diagnostic)\n    (releasing (-> state (dissoc :run-end) (update :diagnostics conj diagnostic))\n               :yin.k/ended (merge {:cause cause} detail) (:lease state))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The candidate loop: propose, observe the grant, accept it whole\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- proposal-request\n  [occurrence pid]\n  {:yin.k/request :yin.k/proposal\n   :yin.k/request-id [:yin.k/proposal occurrence pid]\n   :dao.lease/proposal pid\n   :yin.k/occurrence occurrence})\n\n\n(defn- fresh-pid\n  [_state]\n  (str (random-uuid)))\n\n\n(defn- propose\n  \"Send or resend the current proposal, minting a fresh id when the last\n   one was refused (7.7.8: a proposal id is answered at most once), and\n   answer the state after it: while it is unanswered the step answers\n   `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`.  A minted id's intent record is durable in\n   the journal before its first send, so a resent proposal keeps its\n   stable id across a reopen.  A stopped candidate never proposes\n   again, and an unavailable inbox holds the proposal until a scan\n   succeeds: either way the outstanding proposal waits passively, and\n   a late grant to it is still observed from the ledger.\"\n  [state projection]\n\n    (append!\n      [_ value]\n      (require-tenure! state)\n      (stream/append! handle value))\n\n\n    stream/IDaoStreamClosable\n\n    (close!\n      [_]\n      (require-tenure! state)\n      (stream/close! handle))))\n\n\n(defn- guard-writes\n  [state machine]\n  (reduce (fn [root [path task]]\n            (assoc-in root (conj path :resources)\n                      (into {} (map (fn [[resource handle]]\n                                      [resource\n                                       (if (satisfies? stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor handle)\n                                         (guarded-handle state handle) handle)]))\n                            (:resources task))))\n          machine (machines machine)))\n\n\n(defn- restore-handles\n  [machine original]\n  (reduce (fn [root [path task]]\n            (reduce (fn [root [resource handle]]\n                      (if (satisfies? stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor handle)\n                        (assoc-in root (into path [:resources resource]) handle)\n                        root)) root (:resources task)))\n          machine (machines original)))\n\n\n(defn- run-cycle\n  \"One program cycle of the run, after tenure: the machine's internal\n   computation, replay below the frontier, the writer's emit, the\n   reader's live step.  The unsatisfied answers of the writer and the\n   reader ride the state as :step-unsatisfied; a run end anywhere\n   rides it as :run-end.\"\n  [state]\n  (let [_ (require-tenure! state)\n        m0 (:machine state)\n        m1 (if (and (= :running (vm/gate-mode m0)) (not (:halted? m0)))\n             (vm/run m0)\n             m0)\n        _ (require-tenure! state)\n        replay (reader/replay m1 {:control-outstanding?\n                                  (control-outstanding? state)})\n        m2 (:machine replay)\n        _ (require-tenure! state)\n        request! (fn [request] (require-tenure! state) (append-request! state request))\n        emit (writer/emit (guard-writes state m2) request!\n                          (fn [diagnostic]\n                            (require-tenure! state)\n                            (append-diagnostic! state diagnostic)))\n        m3 (restore-handles (:machine emit) m2)\n        live (reader/step m3 request!\n                          (fn [h op arg]\n                            (require-tenure! state)\n                            (observe! state h op arg)))\n        m4 (:machine live)]\n    (assoc state\n           :machine m4\n           :run-end (or (:run-end replay) (:run-end emit) (:run-end live))\n           :step-unsatisfied (or (:unsatisfied replay)\n                                 (:unsatisfied emit)\n                                 (:unsatisfied live)))))\n\n\n(defn- at-safepoint?\n  \"True when the machine has nothing left the candidate half owes it:\n   halted with no wait entry and no queued work anywhere in the tree.\"\n  [machine]\n  (and (:halted? machine)\n       (every? (fn [[_ m]]\n                 (and (empty? (:wait-set m)) (empty? (:ready-queue m))))\n               (machines machine))))\n\n\n(defn- exit-settled?\n  \"True when the exit's release bracket is closed: the report stands\n   and the release has been carried, so the closure is the grantor's\n   transition and no tenure of this driver's can change it.\"\n  [state]\n  (and (map? (:exit state))\n       (true? (get-in state [:release :carried]))))\n\n\n(defn- cycle-result\n  [state]\n  (let [run-end (:run-end state)\n        unsatisfied-step (:step-unsatisfied state)\n        base (dissoc state :run-end :step-unsatisfied)]\n    (cond\n      (= :releasing (:phase base)) base\n      run-end (end-run base (:cause run-end) (dissoc run-end :cause))\n      (:inbox-unavailable? base) base\n      unsatisfied-step (with-status base :yin.k/unsatisfied\n                         (dissoc unsatisfied-step :yin.k/status))\n      (at-safepoint? (:machine base))\n      (let [{:keys [state refusal]} (arm-exit base :yin.k/result)]\n        (cond-> (assoc state :phase :safepoint)\n          (nil? refusal)\n          (assoc :status :yin.k/ok\n                 :detail {:yin.k/occurrence (:occurrence state)\n                          :dao.lease/lease (:lease state)})))\n      :else (with-status base :yin.k/ok\n              {:yin.k/occurrence (:occurrence base) :dao.lease/lease (:lease base)}))))\n\n\n(defn- step-active-held\n  \"Share binding validation and tenure between control and program scheduling.\n   Only program scheduling applies retained results or prepares an export.\"\n  [state mode]\n  (let [state (if (or (= :program mode) (nil? (:lease state))) state (collect-inbox state))]\n    (cond\n      (:inbox-refused? state) state\n      (= :releasing (:phase state)) state\n      (nil? (:lease state)) state\n      (and (:stopped? state) (nil? (get-in state [:exit :address])))\n      (end-run state :stopped {:dao.lease/lease (:lease state)})\n      (exit-settled? state)\n      (if (= :program mode) state (step-exiting state))\n      :else\n      (let [{checked :state projection :projection} (binding-check state)]\n        (if-not projection\n          checked\n          (let [drained (drain (dissoc checked :program-suspended?)\n                               (if (= :control mode) :control-buffered :program))]\n            (cond\n              (= :releasing (:phase drained)) (dissoc drained :run-end)\n              (:program-suspended? drained) (dissoc drained :program-suspended?)\n              (:run-end drained) (end-run (dissoc drained :run-end)\n                                          (get-in drained [:run-end :cause])\n                                          (dissoc (:run-end drained) :cause))\n              :else\n              (let [reading (clock drained)\n                    _ (require-tenure! drained)\n                    [renewed holder] (if (= :program mode)\n                                       [drained (:holder drained)]\n                                       (renew drained (:holder drained) reading))\n                    state (assoc renewed :holder holder)\n                    armed? (some? (:exit state))\n                    preparing? (nil? (get-in state [:exit :address]))]\n                (cond\n                  (:inbox-unavailable? state)\n                  (if (and armed? (not preparing?) (not= :program mode))\n                    (let [progressed (drain (step-exit-body state projection) mode)]\n                      (if (and (:inbox-unavailable? progressed) (= :yin.k/ok (:status progressed)))\n                        (with-status progressed (:status state) (:detail state))\n                        progressed))\n                    state)\n                  (and armed?\n                       (or (and (= :control mode) preparing?)\n                           (and (= :program mode) (not preparing?)))) state\n                  armed? (drain (step-exit-body state projection) mode)\n                  (or (= :control mode) (= :activating (:phase state)))\n                  (with-status state :yin.k/ok\n                    {:yin.k/occurrence (:occurrence state) :dao.lease/lease (:lease state)})\n                  :else (cycle-result (drain (run-cycle state) mode)))))))))))\n\n\n(defn- step-active\n  \"The held step under the catch: every seam the cycle runs through\n   answers an outcome and never throws (the seam contract of\n   `initial`), so a throw here is the cycle's own -- a tenure guard or\n   a program effect -- and it ends the run.  An uncertain journal\n   append is none of these: it unwinds untouched, and `step` stalls\n   everything on it.\"\n  [state mode]\n  (try (step-active-held state mode)\n       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) failure\n         (if (or (::journal-uncertain (ex-data failure)) (::inbox/refusal (ex-data failure)))\n           (throw failure)\n           (if (= :lease-bound (::cause (ex-data failure)))\n             (end-run state :lease-bound {:dao.lease/lease (:lease state)})\n             (end-run state :program-failure\n                      {:failure {:message (ex-message failure)\n                                 :data (ex-data failure)}}))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The release step\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- step-releasing\n  \"A pending release is retried with the identical request until\n   authenticated carriage is acknowledged, whose acknowledgment is\n   journaled.  A release a restarted holder owes (:re-propose?)\n   re-enters as a candidate once it has left, instead of answering the\n   failure the dead run died with.\"\n  [state]\n  (let [state (drain-control state)\n        release (:release state)]\n    (if (:inbox-refused? state)\n      state\n      (if (true? (:carried release))\n        (if (and (:re-propose? release) (not (:stopped? state)))\n          (-> state\n              (dissoc :release :holder :evidence)\n              (assoc :phase :proposing :proposal nil)\n              (with-status :yin.k/awaiting-grant\n                {:yin.k/occurrence (:occurrence state)}))\n          (-> state\n              (assoc :phase :failed)\n              (update :detail assoc :dao.lease/released true)))\n        (release! state (:lease release))))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '100,177p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc; rg -n 'recovery-metadata|valid-state|ready|recovery-waits' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc; rg -n 'not-quiescent|Status|status table' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1,60p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc; sed -n '310,334p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '100,177p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc; rg -n 'recovery-metadata|valid-state|ready|recovery-waits' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc; rg -n 'not-quiescent|Status|status table' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1,60p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc; sed -n '310,334p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"      {:yin.k/hold :pending-close}\n\n      (some #(and (= :link-request (:reason %)) (not (contains? % :cursor)))\n            waits)\n      {:yin.k/hold :link-cursor-not-installed})))\n\n\n(defn- first-refusal\n  [vm]\n  (some (fn [[path m]]\n          (when (seq (:ready-queue m))\n            {:yin.k/status :yin.k/not-quiescent\n             :yin.k/path path\n             :yin.k/ready (count (:ready-queue m))}))\n        (machines [] vm)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Entering exporting\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn enter\n  \"Enter exporting from `machine`.  Answers\n   `{:status :ok :machine m :record r}`: `m` is `machine` with the gate\n   `:exporting`, an empty wait set and no parked record, and `r` the\n   export record, holding the wait set in order, every parked record\n   (the row carrier carries them all, continuation §2.5), and the gate\n   to return to.  Otherwise answers the data outcome of the first\n   refusal, leaving `machine` as it was: `:yin.k/not-quiescent` for\n   queued work in the root or a child, a `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind\n   `:reason-mismatch` (naming the hold and the task path), or\n   `:yin.k/refused` for a machine that is already exporting or ended.\"\n  [machine]\n  (cond\n    (contains? #{:exporting :ended} (vm/gate-mode machine))\n    (refused :not-running)\n\n    :else\n    (or (first-refusal machine)\n        (some (fn [[path m]]\n                (when-some [h (refusal-at m)]\n                  (non-portable (assoc h :yin.k/path path))))\n              (machines [] machine))\n        {:status :ok\n         :machine (cond-> (assoc machine :yin.k/gate :exporting :wait-set [])\n                    (contains? machine :parked) (assoc :parked {}))\n         :record {:wait-set (vec (:wait-set machine))\n                  :parked (:parked machine)\n                  :gate (vm/gate-mode machine)\n                  :served {}}})))\n\n\n(defn- reinstated\n  \"`machine`, an exporting machine, carrying `record`'s waits and\n   parked records again: the value the lift reads.\"\n  [machine record]\n  (-> (reduce-kv (fn [restored module-name child-record]\n                   (update-in restored [:installs module-name :vm]\n                              reinstated child-record))\n                 machine (or (:children record) {}))\n      (assoc :wait-set (:wait-set record))\n      (update :parked merge (:parked record))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Prepare, then encode\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- check-header!\n  \"A header is nil (the fork lift) or a map carrying the arbitration,\n   the counter and the enrolled set; anything else is a defect of the\n   caller, not a refusal.  The lift judges the rest.\"\n  [header]\n  (when (some? header)\n    (when-not (and (map? header)\n                   (contains? header :yin.k/arbitration)\n                   (contains? header :yin.k/next-op-seq)\n                   (set? (:yin.k/enrolled header)))\n7:   `:yin.k/gate :exporting` on the root, requires an empty ready queue,\n30:   travels.  The pack already carries the whole store, every parked\n110:          (when (seq (:ready-queue m))\n113:             :yin.k/ready (count (:ready-queue m))}))\n131:   `:yin.k/refused` for a machine that is already exporting or ended.\"\n200:  \"handle -> descriptor for every key the retained `table` already\n202:   key's task path: the alias reuse an interrupted prepare had already\n204:   serves a handle the record already answers under another alias.\n248:                     ;; an alias of an already-served key asks again for\n254:                     ;; a handle already served under another resource id\n324:(defn- recovery-metadata\n336:                           (recovery-metadata\n371:                      :yin.k/state (recovery-metadata machine prepared-record)}\n408:(defn- valid-state?\n426:                     (valid-state? child (get-in state [:children mn]))))\n457:                    (:yin.k/recovery-waits machine)\n471:                                      (dissoc :yin.k/recovery-waits :yin.k/recovery-parked\n527:              _ (when-not (valid-state? task state)\n4:> **Status: Proposed / Deferred.** The Semantic VM's linear CESK state is\n27:Status: Phase 5 design draft, 2026-09-14; revised 2026-09-14 (r2) per the\n1673:  (`:yin.k/not-quiescent` otherwise, §7.4.1) — a task with queued runnable\n2632:   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n3140:  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; queued work gives `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n(ns yin.vm.ucf.holder.driver\n  \"The custody driver of M-next D slices D13 and D14 (UCF 7.7.2 to\n   7.7.8, 7.8, 7.9; r3 1.5, 1.6, 1.7, 1.8, 1.9 and 1.10; the D9 header\n   and D10 lower-inputs rulings; linker-dht 14.2.2): one explicit\n   composition-local step state, stepped by the composition, that\n   carries a candidate from bytes to a task under custody, drives that\n   task's run, and -- D14 -- carries the source half (a first export\n   and its offer) and the exit half (the successor, the report, the\n   release and the closure) over a write-ahead progress journal.\n\n   The driver reaches the authority through exactly the three remote\n   paths of r3 1.5, all composition-supplied functions: the front's\n   request stream (`append-request!`), the version-1 positional\n   `reply-inbox` and `outcome-inbox`, and the authenticated ledger\n   reader (`read-ledger!`, whose attributed records D3's\n   `read-evidence` and `custody/binding-evidence` fold).  The lease\n   clock (`clock`) is the composition's too, and dao.lease's own holder\n   discipline -- `observe-grant`, `holding?`, `due-to-renew?`,\n   `observe-renewal`, `stop` -- is the tenure arithmetic over it.\n\n   The phases, one `:phase` key:\n\n   :validating -- fetch and validate with zero side effects: the rows\n   inspector over the bytes and address (`rows.checkpoint/inspect`), then\n   `rows/lower-task` under `:exclusive` with no grant, which refuses\n   before any attachment, so nothing is attached and no machine is\n   exposed: it answers `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` for a valid exclusive\n   blocked or parked root; anything else is a data refusal with no\n   cleanup owed.  A halted exclusive root is not a lease subject: it\n   lowers once, gated `:ended`, and the driver publishes it with no\n   custody.\n\n   :proposing -- check the authority attachment (a missing one is\n   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the arbitration), propose through the\n   front with a fresh proposal id, and observe the grant from the\n   ledger: while the proposal is unanswered the step answers\n   `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`; a grant to another, or an occurrence that\n   can never be granted again, answers `:yin.k/not-holder` with the\n   lease state observed and releases nothing.  A grant to this driver\n   is accepted only whole: `custody/binding-evidence` over the same\n   records (an invalid binding -- none, a duplicate, an inexact epoch,\n   one outside the grant's transaction, one that mismatches its grant\n   -- is `:yin.k/not-holder` followed by a release), then D3's\n   `read-evidence` (unavailable history is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, never\n   an empty prefix, and never a release: custody was not accepted),\n   then the admitted-variant rule (the address must be among the fold's\n   admitted variants of the granted occurrence -- equality of supplied\n   addresses proves nothing), then current tenure: the fold's live\n   lease at the binding's epoch and dao.lease's bound, rechecked before\n   any execution or IO is scheduled.  Only then D10's lower runs, with\n   the checkpoint address, the composition's protection declaration and\n   the grant evidence; a lower failure is after the grant, and releases\n   first.  A regrant replays from the checkpoint: the lower starts\n   input at zero under the evidence's prefix.\n\n   :activating -- an authenticated grant is retained with its original\n   observation basis, without a machine. Control ticks may renew it;\n   only a program tick revalidates and lowers it.\n\n   :running -- run under the gate and drive the writer's and reader's\n    (check! (some? address) \"the address the bytes were fetched under\"\n            {:address address})\n    (check-common! config units)\n    (restore-inbox (bare-state config units)\n                   (:inbox (fold-journal (journal-records (:journal config)))))))\n\n\n(defn source\n  \"The source's composition-local step state over `config`: everything\n   `initial` takes except :bytes and :address -- the driver mints those\n   itself -- plus\n\n     :machine           the machine at its liftable safepoint (parked,\n                        blocked or halted), gate :running; never one\n                        that already holds custody (a holder exports its\n                        successor through `hand-off`)\n     :arbitration       the arbitration medium the composition names, an\n                        exclusive export; nil is the composition's fork\n                        decision: the row lift carries no custody,\n                        nothing is minted, offered or journaled, and the\n                        driver answers the bytes\n\n   The driver enters exporting, mints the occurrence once, journals it\n   before prepare, prepares, encodes, fences and offers; once the offer\n   is admitted it becomes a candidate like any other, over its own\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('docs/agents/known-gaps.md');s=p.read_text().replace('6d-a, 6d-b, 6e; acceptance pending','6d-a landed at d5987015; 6d-b and 6e pending');s += '\\\\n| A module-exported host function held in a window mid-application refuses lift unless the composition registers it in \"'`:primitives`: an honest refusal and an any-pc hole | Fable 6d-a sign-off, d5987015 | yin.vm.semantic | 6e or a later slice: lift module exports by module coordinate via host-export profiles | P3 |'\"\\\\n';p.write_text(s)\np=Path('src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc');s=p.read_text().replace('and a child\\\\n   that is blocked: the engine links a halted child in the sweep it\\\\n   halts in.', 'and a child\\\\n   that is blocked at phase :parked or running at phase :running: the\\\\n   engine links a halted child in the sweep it halts in.');p.write_text(s)\np=Path('test/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc');s=p.read_text().replace('          (is (zero? @repeated))','          ;; Per-group agreement is live only for non-projected vectors\\\\n          ;; (6e\\\\'s hand-built cases).\\\\n          (is (zero? @repeated))');p.write_text(s)\np=Path('docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md');s=p.read_text();anchor='## 7.9';i=s.find(anchor);print('7.9 offset',i)\n# The removed status applies only to the holder pending this slice.\ns += '\\\\n<\"'!-- 6d-a follow-up: :yin.k/not-quiescent remains holder-only until 6d-b replaces the export Quiesce step; the row carrier already retains completed outcomes. -->'\"\\\\n';p.write_text(s)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
