{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_60","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1987,2015p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '1896,1955p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2623,2645p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2935,2965p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '3110,3143p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1667,1680p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_60","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1987,2015p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '1896,1955p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2623,2645p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2935,2965p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '3110,3143p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1667,1680p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":" :wait-set entries}\n```\n\nRestore the ready queue in carried order using the task’s fresh resource keys. Restore each completion’s correlation and terminal-outcome fields. Nothing runs, polls or re-appends during lower. Fenced restoration retains the queue outside the scheduler, and the closed-gate guard prevents execution through every semantic execution entry.\n\nEvery kind restores slot 18 in order. Resolve the ready state and completion descriptor, reconstruct the engine entry described in §3.1, then restore its value, status, refusal and resource updates. Update keys map through the slot-9 C→fresh-resource-key map; update bodies come from the ready row, not from slot 9.\n\nNo completed operation is polled or reissued by lower. A completed FFI request append resumes through the existing response-wait transition. Link/install completion restores without resurrecting retired pending resources.\n\nA fenced restore retains ready entries in `:yin.k/recovery-ready`, alongside recovery waits and parked records, and exposes an empty scheduler queue. Running control remains available for byte-identical relift, protected by the 6d-a closed-gate guard.\n\n\n### 7.3 Custody and lifecycle of a running root\n\n**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\n\nAn exclusive running, blocked or parked root carries policy, occurrence, arbitration and next-op-seq, with origin on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries origin only. A completed `:put` wake is a completed operation: its op-id is well-formed but not a retained write. Completed outcomes never acquire publication or execution authority from their history.\n\nStage-local execution returns the latest completed holder state explicitly on tenure failure. The ended holder retains that machine for inspection only. Recovery uses the last durable checkpoint; progress since it is lost. Inner immutable rebinding is not a channel to an outer catch.\n\nProgress since the last durable checkpoint is lost on expiry; fuel makes the computation interruptible, exporting before the bound is what preserves it. Stage-local holder transitions, tenure cuts and durable export are implemented and tested by 6d-b. The 6d-a carrier admits running custody subjects and retains fenced recovery data.\n\nDirect `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel` refuse `:exporting` and `:ended` before guest, scheduler or bridge work. The guard is outside the instruction loop. The refusal is an ex-info whose data is `{:rule :gate-closed :yin.k/gate mode}` (`mode` the closed gate); 6d-b's holder paths reuse this name. A fenced running restore preserves control, window, environment, K and ordered completed outcomes for byte-identical relift; nothing runs during lower.\n\n### 7.4 Fuel\n\n**`yin.vm.semantic/run-fuel [machine n]`**, public, with positive exact integer `n`.\n\nOne invocation performs:\ndirect `:resume`, and may retry a blocked write, all after the value is in\nother machines' hands. Delaying register restoration is too late; the\nappend is the effect.\n\nUCF therefore defines an explicit **exporting** transition that precedes\nany publication, and the lift driver performs it as one step over the\nmachine value:\n\n1. **Quiesce**: the ready-queue must be empty (§7.6.3).\n2. **Detach the wait set**: every wait entry moves from the machine's\n   wait-set into the export record. The machine's wait-set is now empty,\n   so *there is nothing to poll* — no poll, no append, no effect. The\n   entries are not lost: the export record retains them verbatim; they are\n   the source of the `:yin.k/pending` variants of §7.4.3.\n3. **Neutralize control**: the record's control and K are already absent\n   (the machine is parked or blocked); direct `:resume` resolves against\n   `:parked`, and the parked records referenced by reachable code travel\n   inside the export record — a local `:resume` against them is refused\n   while exporting (the restore function observes the exporting machine\n   and declines), because the parked id now names a checkpoint that may be\n   granted elsewhere.\n4. **Publish, then offer**: append segments and the value body; record\n   `:yin.k/offered` on the arbitration medium. Only now do readers exist,\n   and the source was fenced before any of them did.\n\nThe exporting machine is pure data with an empty wait-set and a retained\nexport record — no engine change, no callback, no timer; the drivers of the\nworld observe the state as they observe `ready-for-ingress?` (§3.1), and a\ndriver that polls an exporting machine's wait set anyway is a host assembly\ndefect of the same kind as wiring a refusing deposit destination.\n\n**Failure recovery and idempotent retry.** If publication fails — a full\ncarrier, an unreachable arbitration medium — the machine stays exporting\nand the composition retries; a retry re-encodes or reuses the encoded value\nand **reuses the occurrence** (§7.3.4), so no retry mints a second runnable\ncheckpoint. Abandoning the export reinstates the export record into the\nmachine (wait entries return to the wait-set, parked records to `:parked`)\nand the task resumes as a purely local one — the sole paths back to local\nexecution are *abort export* (before the offer is recorded) and *a grant to\nthe emitter itself* (after); there is no third.\n\n(M-next D8/D9, as built.) The exporting machine carries one gate key,\n`:yin.k/gate`, with the modes `:running`, `:exporting` and `:ended`:\nunder `:running` internal computation proceeds and stream observations\nand effects are deferred to the driver; under `:exporting` and `:ended`\nnothing is observed, no install child is advanced, a direct resume is\nrefused, and every public apply refuses a late result. The root's mode\nis stamped on each install child at its creation and before each run;\na child carries the mode only. A machine entering exporting refuses\nthe custody holds -- a wait entry with reason `:observe`, an entry or\ncursor cell carrying `:yin.k/held`, a cursor cell carrying `:yin.k/unminted`,\na machine with a nonempty `:yin.k/closes` queue, and a\n`:link-request` entry without `:cursor` -- as `:yin.k/non-portable`,\nkind `:reason-mismatch`, with the holding task's path; the lift\nrefuses the same holds on its own behalf. A task is not at a liftable\nsafepoint while it holds any of these.\n\n**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the holder now exports rows with a local header and source index.\nThe lift's custody input is one **header** argument -- supplied to\n`holder.export/prepare [machine record serve! header source-index]`,\nthan waiting forever. The repair is prospective: no retroactive standalone\nclosure transaction over such old history is authorized. Repeated reopen neither\nduplicates an edge nor advances an already-reclaimed lease's epoch again.\n\n## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\n\n**Lift** (emitter side; composition-driven, never automatic on every park):\n\n1. The machine is parked or blocked with a local record `r`, and its\n   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n2. **Enter exporting** (§7.7.4): detach the wait set into the export\n   record, neutralize direct `:resume`. From here the source is fenced; no\n   poll, retry, or local resume can perform an effect.\n3. `lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at a safepoint (§7.4.1),\n   else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; canonicalize every referenced segment and\n   compute its address (§7.3.2), else the refusal; encode (§7.5), else the\n   refusal; compute `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` by the fixed point\n   (§7.6), recording the discovery status; stamp the contract (§7.3.3); on\n   first export, mint the occurrence; mint `:yin.k/id`.\n4. Append the canonical instruction vectors of every required segment to a\n   `dao.jing` intake pool (the payloads *are* the vectors, §7.3.4), then\n   the id-less value body to the carrier medium; observe `:dao.stream/ok`\n   on each. `:yin.k/carried` may then be empty. Retry of any failed append\n  cross-activation-`E` cases; module manifests publishing store footprints\n  so `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` disappears.\n- **Isolated-store lowering.** Conformance tests that the resumed task's\n  store contains exactly the slice and the resumer's pair, and that a\n  receiver-side binding of a name in the slice's name set cannot change\n  resolution.\n- **Safepoint conformance harness.** A test that runs every corpus segment\n  on the reference machine to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table —\n  effect-producing calls included — lifts, lowers into a fresh VM, and\n  checks the parity suite's result; the same harness is what a foreign\n  engine runs to claim conformance, including `lift ∘ lower = id` on\n  canonical frames, the static `def`/`use`/`live` and\n  `lexically-required` checks, the frame validator on every frame, and\n  **cross-engine canonical equality**: one program at one safepoint lifts\n  to row sets with equal root ids after the harness's specified\n  re-emission (`yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md` §5.5) from\n  the canonical semantic kernel and from the stack and register backends\n  through their layouts, the stack kernel with its tail-completion seam.\n  **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14; names updated at the 2026-10-15 cutover)** — Reason: the owner retains both kernels as backends on a fixed fragment.\n  The canonical kernel and its two backends are checked on the\n  documented fragment and required to refuse the\n  unsupported remainder before attach (continuation design §4.6, the retained\n  backend fragment, including the `:retained-ffi` pre-attach refusal and\n  remaining harness-only exclusions; there is no general pre-attach fragment gate). They are retained\n  after phase 8 without widening the fragment;\n  and the §7.3.4 image-equality assertion (the two AST projection lanes\n  and the direct vector load yield one image and address).\n- **Exporting-state integration.** The lift driver's exporting transition\n  and its failure/retry paths, tested specifically against the\n  poll-a-blocked-writer-appends hazard of §7.7.4.\n\n**Safepoint reconstruction (7.4; blocker 1).**\n\n- Invariant: At every declared parking transition, lifting and lowering\n  preserve the next observable step and its wait, while other pcs refuse.\n- Setup: Run the corpus to every 7.4.1 row: explicit park, blocked\n  stream read, blocked write, sent FFI, retained FFI, ordinary and tail\n  effectful calls, and halt; also exercise M4's `:link-request`,\n  `:link-response`, and `:install` pending variants (linker section 11\n  item 12). Exercise distinct activation depths and captured environments\n  at one pc, a nonempty ready queue, a nonsafepoint pc, and a kept response\n  cursor shared by two waiters. Omit the portable cursor profile for the\n  refusal case.\n  **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: cross-engine conformance covers the documented backend fragment.\n  Separately prepare the canonical kernel and its two backends\n  (semantic; register; stack after its tail-completion seam),\n  within continuation design §4.6's fragment, with\n  a nontrivial call stack, a captured closure, and a blocked effect at\n  the handoff point.\n- Action and expected outcome: On each host, lift a parked task and lower\n  into a fresh VM, then compare its result and effect trace with the\n  reference machine. Before the full corpus, lift that handoff from one\n  engine and lower it in the other; the canonical row set after\n  re-emission, the pending wait, next result and effect trace must agree\n  across all three and in every lowering direction. Static\n  `:yin.safepoint/kinds`, `def`/`use`/`live`, and lexical reads agree with\n  the reference trace. The observed parked record, wait entry, and effect\n  outcome determine `:yin.k/reason` and `:yin.k/pending`, never the\n  static kind alone.\n  Test both FFI states and each call effect. `lift(lower(frame))` must\n  reproduce the canonical frame. An undeclared pc gives\n  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; queued work gives `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n  Insufficient pending evidence refuses before publication, as does a\n  missing portable cursor profile. Halt yields a result, not a frame.\n- Reflection acceptance (`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`, slice 8): Park a\n`engine/resume-continuation`, §3.5). UCF therefore states the unit and\ncarries the state:\n\n- **The unit is the whole blocked machine**: registers (the frame), the\n  wait-set (as pending variants, §7.4.3), the referenced `:parked` records,\n  and the fresh-name counter. The ready-queue must be empty at lift\n  (`:yin.k/not-quiescent` otherwise, §7.4.1) — a task with queued runnable\n  work has a next configuration that is not the parked one. **Amended\n  2026-10-16 (any-pc):** for the register-shaped machine the unit also\n  carries an active run state and the ordered ready queue as completed\n  outcomes (§7.4.1 amendment; continuation design §2.3); there is no\n  quiescence refusal.\n- **Fresh-name state travels.** `:yin.k/scheduler {:yin.k/id-counter n\n  …}` carries the counter verbatim; the lowered machine adopts\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_61","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat > /tmp/6db-docs.py <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md');s=p.read_text()\ns=s.replace('1. **Quiesce**: the ready-queue must be empty (§7.6.3).', '1. **Retain the completed outcomes.** Enter the exporting gate and move the ordered ready queue into the retained export record. The live scheduler queue is empty. Preserve every outcome value, status, completion descriptor, cursor update and raised-refusal value. No queued operation is executed, discarded or reissued by this transition.')\na=s.index('3. **Neutralize control**:');b=s.index('4. **Publish, then offer**:',a)\ns=s[:a]+'''3. **Neutralize control:** a parked or blocked machine has no control; a machine between instructions (root kind \"'`:running`) keeps its `:control`, `:window`, `:env` and `:k` as the state the lift reads. The semantic execution entries refuse an exporting or ended machine before instruction, scheduler or bridge execution. This guard ships with running fenced restoration in 6d-a and remains outside the instruction loop. Direct `:resume` resolves against the parked records in the retained export record and is refused while exporting.\n'\"'''+s[b:]\ns=s.replace('pure data with an empty wait-set and a retained\\\\nexport record — no engine change, no callback, no timer', 'pure data with its wait set, ready queue and parked records moved into the retained export record — one engine change at the kernel’s entry (the gate guard), no change to the instruction loop, no callback, no timer')\ns=s.replace('under \"'`:running` internal computation proceeds and stream observations'\"', 'under \"'`:running` internal computation proceeds for the holder’s fuel budget per program tick (`driver/run-cycle`, `:fuel`), so a long computation is preempted at an instruction boundary and may be exported there; a holder whose tenure ends receives the latest completed state through stage-local failure handling, retains it for inspection, and offers nothing. Recovery remains the last durable checkpoint. Progress since it is lost (continuation design §7.3). Stream observations'\"')\ns=s.replace('A task is not at a liftable\\\\nsafepoint while it holds any of these.', 'A task holding one of these incomplete driver transitions is not export-ready. Resolution may require repeated driver cycles, external acknowledgement or relief from backpressure; no fixed cycle count is promised.')\ns=s.replace('runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks', 'runs to its next park, halt, or fuel boundary')\ns=s.replace('1. The machine is parked or blocked with a local record \"'`r`, and its'\"\\\\n   ready-queue is empty, else \"'`:yin.k/not-quiescent`.'\"', '1. The machine is parked, blocked, halted or between instructions (\"'`:running`), with a local record `r`, and holds no observation in flight, else `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch`.'\"')\ns=s.replace('confirm the record is at a safepoint (§7.4.1),\\\\n   else', 'confirm every pc is a pc of A (§7.4.1),\\\\n   else')\ns=s.replace('At the next safepoint or halt, produce the successor occurrence', 'At the next park, halt, or fuel boundary, produce the successor occurrence')\ns=s.replace('Resume happens only at a safepoint the machine\\\\'s parking transitions define;', 'Resume happens only at an instruction boundary the canonical vector defines (a parking transition, or the boundary a holder’s fuel budget stopped at);')\ns=s.replace(\\\"The engine's hot loop is untouched: lifting is a pure function over the blocked machine's value, and the exporting state is that value with an emptied wait-set and a retained record — driver discipline, not engine machinery.\\\", 'The engine’s instruction loop is untouched: lifting is a pure function over the machine’s value at any boundary, the one engine change is a gate check at the kernel’s entry, and the exporting state is that value with its waits, queue and parked records moved into a retained record — driver discipline, not engine machinery.')\ns=s.replace('**Safepoint conformance harness.**', '**Continuation conformance harness.**').replace('to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table —\\\\n  effect-producing calls included —', 'to **every boundary of the stepping driver** (continuation design §5.7), the complete §7.4.1 table’s parking transitions included,').replace('one program at one safepoint lifts', 'one program at one aligned instruction boundary lifts')\ns=s.replace('Invariant: At every declared parking transition, lifting and lowering\\\\n  preserve the next observable step and its wait, while other pcs refuse.', 'Invariant: At every instruction boundary, lifting and lowering preserve the next observable step (and, at a parking transition, its wait; with a non-empty queue, its order); a pc outside every body refuses.')\ns=s.replace('a nonsafepoint pc', 'a \"'`:push` pc of the stack image (relabels) and a body-start pc (lifts)'\"')\ns=s.replace('An undeclared pc gives\\\\n  \"'`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; queued work gives `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.'\"', 'A pc outside every body gives \"'`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; every pc of A lifts as a `run` state when the machine is between instructions; queued work travels as `:ready` rows and resumes in order.'\"')\ns=s.replace('blocked/parked exclusive', 'running/blocked/parked exclusive')\ns=s.replace('\\\\n<\"'!-- 6d-a follow-up: :yin.k/not-quiescent remains holder-only until 6d-b replaces the export Quiesce step; the row carrier already retains completed outcomes. -->'\"\\\\n', '\\\\n')\np.write_text(s)\np=Path('docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md');s=p.read_text().replace('| Explicit park | No active wait;', '| Between instructions (:running) | Active control; ordered waits and completed wakes travel; the window is exactly L(pc); continue at pc. |\\\\n| Explicit park | No active wait;').replace('Static \"'`:yin.safepoint/kinds` checks pc eligibility only.'\"', 'Static \"'`:yin.safepoint/kinds` checks a parking pc’s kinds; a `run` pc is any pc of a body.'\"').replace('Validate the safepoint and empty ready queue; enter exporting before', 'Validate the instruction boundary (§7.4.1 as amended); enter exporting before').replace('continue to the next safepoint.', 'continue to the next park, halt or fuel boundary.').replace('A non-safepoint pc and queued work\\\\n  yield the existing not-at-safepoint and not-quiescent outcomes.', 'A pc outside every body yields \"'`not-at-safepoint`; queued work travels as `:ready` rows.'\"').replace('At the next safepoint or halt,', 'At the next park, halt, or fuel boundary,');p.write_text(s)\np=Path('docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md');s=p.read_text();a=s.index('Stage-local execution returns');b=s.index('\\\\n### 7.4 Fuel',a);s=s[:a]+'''1. **Preemption under live tenure.** \"'`run-cycle` uses `(if fuel (sr/run-fuel m0 fuel) (vm/run m0))` under its existing gate and halted guards. Holder `:fuel` is a positive integer or nil, checked by `check-common!`. Nil retains unrestricted behavior. After replay, emit and live read, active control leaves the holder running with `:yin.k/at-boundary true` in its status detail. The composition explicitly calls `hand-off`; no automatic export at the bound is implied.\n\n2. **Tenure dies after a completed slice.** Stage-local execution returns the latest completed holder state explicitly on tenure failure. The ended holder retains that machine for inspection only. Recovery uses the last durable checkpoint; progress since it is lost. Inner immutable rebinding is not a channel to an outer catch.\n\n   Each stage checks tenure against its input, executes with the existing external-seam checks, associates its returned machine and metadata, and passes that successor to the next stage. Its outcome is `{:status :ok :state completed-state}` or `{:status :failed :state latest-completed-state :failure failure}`. The stage-local catch closes over its input. The failure consumer calls `end-run` on that carried state; `:yin.k/retained-boundary` records its PC, segment, checkpoint and completed-slice count. A failure before an operation returns retains the previous completed stage, never an invented partial result. External effects are not rolled back. Journal-uncertain and inbox-refusal exceptions retain their propagation rules.\n\n3. **Expiry and recovery.** Progress since the last durable checkpoint is lost on expiry; fuel makes the computation interruptible, exporting before the bound is what preserves it. Recovery lowers the checkpoint this holder received, or a durably authorized successor. Retained local progress receives no adoption, execution or publication authority. The source remains ended; `exit-mint` and abort cannot revive its tenure.\n\n4. **Reopen and fencing.** Direct `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel` refuse `:exporting` and `:ended` before guest, scheduler or bridge work. The guard ships with running fenced restoration in 6d-a and remains outside the instruction loop. The refusal is an ex-info whose data is `{:rule :gate-closed :yin.k/gate mode}`. A fenced running restore preserves control, window, environment, K and ordered completed outcomes for byte-identical relift; nothing runs during lower. `rehydrate-fenced` reproduces the published pack byte for byte, including cursor updates.\n\n'\"'''+s[b:];p.write_text(s)\nPY\npython3 /tmp/6db-docs.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
